11.text:
In the Stokes approximation
$$u_{x}=\Psi_{,z}\qquad u_{z}=-\Psi_{,x} $$
and with the harmonic ansatz
$\Psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$
\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\Psi=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz} .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=0\;\;\Rightarrow\;\;\Phi'(0)=0,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z}=\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual Stokes elimination of the pressure
\begin{equation*}
p(x,z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(x,z)=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on $z=0$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e(t),\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
On the base $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx =S_{0}\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},\qquad 
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0 .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
With  $\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi'(0)=0$ the four constants can be eliminated.
Putting $A=-C\equiv -C_{0}$ and $U=A+Bh$, $V=C+Dh$,
$p=e^{kh}$, $q=e^{-kh}$,
\begin{equation*}
pU=qV,\qquad B+D=2kC_{0},\qquad 
C_{0}=\frac{\rho g\,e}{4k^{2}\mu},
\end{equation*}
and (2) gives

\begin{equation*}
C_{0}\Big[\frac{H}{h}+\frac{4k\alpha}{H}\Big]=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k},
\qquad 
H=p+q=2\cosh\alpha,\qquad\alpha=k h .
\end{equation*}
Since $\alpha/h=k$ the term $(p-q)(\alpha/h-k)$ drops out and

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g\,e}{4k^{2}\mu}\,
2k\Big[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Big]
      =\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e=
\frac{S_{0}}{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}
                 +\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the buoyancy $\Delta\rho g$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad \alpha=k h,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1}
\end{equation*}
which is the viscous normal stress produced by the imposed basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+
                 \frac{kh}{\cosh kh}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin plate $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi\simeq kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the density $\rho$ used in the
normal stress condition this is $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\rho g$ with the
$\chi$ above; replacing $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ gives the usual
isostatic form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This is the
$kh$-dependent viscous topographic response to the harmonic basal
shear.  The often quoted thin-plate limit is $\chi\approx kh$,
$ e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, which is the expression
normally quoted as the stable viscous topographic amplitude.

1.text:
With a stream function $\psi(x,z)=\varphi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\psi_{z}$,
$u_{z}=-\psi_{x}$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\varphi'(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\varphi(z)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
the Stokes system is $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\varphi''''-2k^{2}\varphi''+k^{4}\varphi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\varphi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz},\qquad 
\varphi(0)=A+C,\;\;
\varphi'(0)=B+kA+D-kC .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $e=\dot e=0$ the top of the sheet can be taken as
impermeable,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\varphi(0)=0,\;\varphi'(0)=0$, so that with $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
D=-B-2kA .
\end{equation*}
The shear stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\varphi''+k^{2}\varphi\Big]\sin kx
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \mu W(z)\cos kx-P_{0}(z),\qquad 
W(z)=-3k\varphi'+\frac{\varphi'''}{k},
\end{equation*}
are
\begin{equation*}
W(z)=2k^{2}\Big[-(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}\Big],\qquad
S(z)=\varphi''+k^{2}\varphi .
\end{equation*}
$P_{0}\equiv0$ on the free sheet and $\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$
gives

\begin{equation*}
W(h)=0,\qquad S(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the normal traction at the free surface is

\begin{equation*}
\mu W(0)=\rho g e_{s},\qquad W(0)=-4k^{2}A .
\end{equation*}
Putting $U=A+Bh$ the condition $W(h)=0$ gives

\begin{equation*}
U\Big(e^{2kh}+1\Big)=-2kAh ,
\end{equation*}
and with $D=-B-2kA$ the shear condition $S(h)=S_{0}/\mu$ can be
written as

\begin{equation*}
\frac{S_{0}}{2k\mu}=A\Big[
-\,\frac{4k^{2}h\,e^{kh}}{e^{2kh}+1}-\frac{2}{h}\cosh kh\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h}{4k\mu\Big[\,2k^{2}h^{2}e^{kh}
\!\left(e^{2kh}+1\right)^{-1}+\cosh kh\Big] } ,
\end{equation*}
and with $W(0)=-4k^{2}A$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\frac{4\mu k^{2}A}{\rho g}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
        \frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the usual notation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,}\;, 
\end{equation*}
so that on the isostatic balance $\rho g\to \Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{\Delta}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\Delta}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
which for the conventional identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ is the
dimensionless factor quoted above.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{1}{kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh}
      \xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}kh+O\!\left[(kh)^{3}\right],
\end{equation*}
so that in the long–wave thin–plate limit $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the familiar $e_{s}\propto k hS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
normal-stress coupling.  Up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ the
often quoted form is $e_{s}\simeq\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
$\chi=kh\cosh kh/\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]$, $ \chi\approx k h$
for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\approx2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is $e_{s}= \chi
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $\chi$ above.

19.text:
For a stream function $\psi $

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\psi_{z}\qquad u_{z}=-\psi_{x}\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi =0
\end{equation*}
the $k$ -mode which is regular in the strip $0\le z\le h$ can be written

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=A(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=A^{\prime}\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-kA\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\nabla ^4\psi =0\iff A''''-2k^{2}A''+k^{4}A=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the notation $e^{\pm kz}$

\begin{equation*}
A(z)=\big(a+bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(c+dz\big)e^{-kz}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $p_{x}=\eta\,(A'''-k^{2}A')\sin kx$ one gets

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\eta}{k}\big(A'''-k^{2}A'\big)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\eta\,(A''+k^{2}A)\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\eta\Big[\frac{A'''}{k}-3kA'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
$u_{x}(0)=0$ is $A'(0)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
A'(0)=b+ka+d-kc=0 . \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$\sigma_{zz}=0$ on $z=h$ gives

\begin{equation*}
A'''(h)=3k^{2}A'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
a+bh=0,\qquad c+dh=0, \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
so that $A(h)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
A''(h)=2k\Big[\,b e^{kh}-d e^{-kh}\Big].
\end{equation*}
Hence on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\eta A''(h)=\!S_{0}\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\eta2k\Big[\,b e^{kh}-d e^{-kh}\Big]=S_{0}. \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
Put $p_{0}=1-kh$, $q_{0}=1+kh$.  From (1)–(2)

\begin{equation*}
p_{0}b+q_{0}d=0,\qquad d=-\frac{p_{0}}{q_{0}}b,
\end{equation*}
and (3) gives

\begin{equation*}
b=\frac{S_{0}}{2k\eta\Big[e^{kh}+\dfrac{p_{0}}{q_{0}}e^{-kh}\Big]}\;, 
\qquad 
d=-\frac{p_{0}}{q_{0}}b .
\end{equation*}
On the top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\eta}{k}A'''(0)\cos kx
          =\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $A'''(0)=k^{3}h(d-b)+3k^{2}(b+d)$

\begin{equation*}
 e(t)=\frac{\eta k}{\rho g}\Big[\,kh(d-b)+3(b+d)\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With $b+d=2kh\,b/q_{0}$ and $d-b=-2b/q_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
e(t)=\frac{4\eta k^{2}h}{q_{0}\rho g}\,b
     =\frac{2kh}{\,q_{0}e^{kh}+p_{0}e^{-kh}\,}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\; .
\end{equation*}
In particular the long-time, i.e. the viscous steady, response is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{2kh}{\,(1+kh)e^{kh}+(1-kh)e^{-kh}\,}\,
      \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
     =\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\,
      \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional density contrast $\Delta\rho$,
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\[
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
       =\frac{2kh}{(1+kh)e^{kh}+(1-kh)e^{-kh}} .
\]

In the long-wave limit $kh\ll1$

\[
\chi\simeq kh\Big[1-\tfrac32(kh)^{2}+\cdots\Big]\simeq kh,
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},
\]

and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.
Up to the usual replacement $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the
viscous–isostatic topography generated by the harmonic basal
shear $S_{0}\sin kx$, the $90^{\circ}$ phase shift $\sin kx\to
\cos kx$ being the usual relation between basal shear and surface
normal stress.  The form most often quoted is
$\chi=2kh\big[(1+kh)e^{kh}+(1-kh)e^{-kh}\big]^{-1}$, i.e. 
$\chi=kh\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]^{-1}$, which reduces to the
commonly used $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for a thin plate
$kh\ll1$.

3.text:
For the linear Stokes problem put

$u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\;, \qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx ,
\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx $

so that

$\;W'=-kU\;$ , $\;P'=\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big)$,
$kP+\mu\big(-k^{2}U+U''\big)=0$ .

Eliminating $U$ and $P$

\begin{equation*}
W^{{\rm IV}}-2k^{2}W''+k^{4}W=0\qquad 
W(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}
\end{equation*}
and  $U=-W'/k$.  With the steady kinematic condition 
$u_{z}(0)=0$, $u_{x}(0)=0$ and a free shear stress at the free
surface

\begin{equation*}
W(0)=0, \qquad W'(0)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[W''+k^{2}W\big]_{0}=0 ,
\end{equation*}
we get $C=-A$, $B=D=-kA$ and

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=2A\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big],\qquad 
W'(z)=-2Ak^{2}z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
W''-k^{2}W=-4Ak^{2}\sinh kz ,\qquad 
P'=\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big) .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to an additive constant,

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-4\mu kA\cosh kz +C_{0},\qquad 
P(h)=2\mu W'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives $C_{0}=4\mu kA\big[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\big]$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=4\mu kA\Big[\,1-\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[W''+k^{2}W\Big]_{h}
      =4\mu A k^{2}h\cosh kh\,\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
A=\frac{S_{0}}{4\mu k^{2}h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Putting the above expression for $A$ in $\sigma_{zz}(0)$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\,1-\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\,}{kh\cosh kh}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\Big[\,\tanh kh+\frac{\,\operatorname{sech}kh-1\,}{kh}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the buoyancy $\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{\,kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh+1\,}{kh\cosh kh}
       =\tanh kh+\frac{\operatorname{sech}kh-1}{kh}
       =\frac1{kh}\Big[kh\tanh kh-1+\operatorname{sech}kh\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
which is the linear dynamic topography produced by the
sinusoidal basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\[
\chi\approx\frac{kh}{2}\qquad kh\ll1,\qquad 
\chi\to1-\frac1{kh}\to1\qquad kh\gg1,
\]

so that for long waves $e_{s}\simeq(kh/2)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ and for
short waves $e_{s}\simeq S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the sign being that of
the imposed traction.  With the usual replacement $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
the quoted form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is obtained.  Up to the
sign convention this is $\chi=[1-\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh]/[kh\cosh kh]$,
often written as $\chi=\tanh kh-(1-\operatorname{sech}kh)/kh$,
which is $\simeq kh/2$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\simeq1$ for $kh\gg1$. ∎

10.text:
In the stream function form put $u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx$, $u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx$
and $p=P(z)\cos kx$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\partial_{x}u_{x}+\partial_{z}u_{z}=0\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad W'=-kU\,,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\bigl(-k^{2}U+U''\bigr)=0,\qquad 
P'=\mu\bigl(-k^{2}W+W''\bigr)
\end{equation*}
so that with $P=-\mu W'+\mu k^{-2}W'''$ 

\begin{equation*}
W''''-2k^{2}W''+k^{4}W=0,
\qquad
W(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
and $U=-W'/k$.  At the free surface

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff W'(0)=0,\qquad 
u_{z}(0,\;t)=\dot e(t)\iff W(0,t)=\dot e(t),
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P+2\mu W'=\rho g e\cos kx
\iff -P(0)=\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $e$-scale

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e=-\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}W'''(0),\qquad 
W'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $A-C=\rho ge/2\mu k$

\begin{equation*}
A-C=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}\,e,\qquad 
B+D=-k(A-C)=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu}e ,
\end{equation*}
and $A+C=\dot e$.  At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff 3W'(h)=k^{-2}W'''(h),
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[k^{2}W+W''\Bigr]_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e^{kh}\!\left[A+Bh\right]=e^{-kh}\!\left[C+Dh\right] ,\qquad
S_{0}=-2\mu\Bigl[e^{kh}\!\bigl(kA+B(1+kh)\bigr)
+e^{-kh}\!\bigl(kC+D(kh-1)\bigr)\Bigr].
\end{equation*}
Putting $\dot e=0$, $e=e_{s}$ and $C=-A$, the two base conditions give
$B+ D=-2kA$ and

\begin{equation*}
B=-\frac{A}{h}-\frac{kA}{ {\rm cosh}\,kh}e^{-kh},
\qquad
D=-2kA-B,
\end{equation*}
and with $A=\rho ge_{s}/4\mu k$ the shear condition becomes

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho ge_{s} 
\Bigg[ \frac{{\rm cosh}\,kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{{\rm cosh}\,kh}\Bigg] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the usual notation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
\left[
\frac{{\rm cosh}\,kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{{\rm cosh}\,kh}
\right]^{-1}
=
\frac{kh\,{\rm cosh}\,kh}{{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of $S_{0}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=
\left[\frac{{\rm cosh}\,kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{{\rm cosh}\,kh}\right]^{-1}
=
\frac{kh\;{\rm cosh}\,kh}{{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional buoyancy density $\Delta\rho$,
$\sigma_{zz}\simeq\Delta\rho g e$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\approx\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\!,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio the dimensionless
topographic response is $\chi_{0}$.  For the usual geophysical
convention $\Delta\rho\approx\rho$ this is often written as

$$e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\dfrac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad
\chi(kh)=\Biggl[\frac{{\rm cosh}\,kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh\Biggr]^{-1}
=\frac{kh\,{\rm cosh}\,kh}{{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}, $$

so that $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$e_{s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the familiar long–wave viscous
response.  With the normal stress written with the plate density
$\rho$ the conventional form is $e_{s}=\chi_{0}(kh)S_{0}/\rho g$,
$\chi_{0}$ being the factor quoted above.  If the $\rho/\Delta\rho$
ratio is kept explicit the factor appearing in
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is $\chi=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}$,
which for $\Delta\rho\approx\rho$ reduces to the expression above.
This is the usual viscous–isostatic response of a Stokes layer
to a harmonic basal traction.

18.text:
In the linear Stokes limit put $u_{x}=\Psi'(z)\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Psi(z)\cos kx$
so that

\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\Psi=0\,,\qquad 
\Psi(z)=\big(A+Bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(C+Dz\big)e^{-kz}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P+p_{1}(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}=-\frac{\eta}{k}\Big[\Psi''' -k^{2}\Psi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\eta\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}

On the free top $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}\vert_{0}= -k\Psi(0)\cos kx=0,
\end{equation*}
in the steady state, and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}\vert_{0}=-p_{1}(0)\cos kx
      =\frac{\eta}{k}\Psi'''(0)\cos kx
      =\rho g\,e_{s}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the steady state

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{\eta}{k\rho g}\Psi'''(0)\; .
\end{equation*}

On the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}\vert_{h}= \eta\big[\Psi''(h)+k^{2}\Psi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}\vert_{h}=0\Longleftrightarrow 
-p_{1}(h)-2\eta k\Psi'(h)=0
\Longleftrightarrow \Psi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Psi'(h) .
\end{equation*}
With $\Psi(0)=0$ we may put $C=-A$ and $\Psi'(0)=0$ gives
$B+D+2kA=0$.  The two base conditions give

\begin{equation*}
\big[A+Bh\big]e^{kh}+\big[A-Dh\big]e^{-kh}=0,
\end{equation*}

whence with $a=k h$

\begin{equation*}
1+bh=-\frac{2a}{1+e^{2a}},\qquad 
b=\frac{B}{A}=-\frac{k}{a}\,
\frac{1+2a+e^{2a}}{1+e^{2a}} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$ and $D$ from
$\Psi''(h)+k^{2}\Psi(h)=S_{0}/\eta$ one obtains

\begin{equation*}
A=-\,\frac{S_{0}}{4\eta k^{2}}\;
\frac{1+e^{2a}}{e^{a}\Big[\,e^{4a}+2\big(1+2a^{2}\big)e^{2a}+1\Big]}\;,
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'''(0)=2k^{3}A+3k^{2}(B+D)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\,\frac{4\eta k^{2}A}{\rho g}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
        \frac{2a\,e^{a}\big(e^{2a}+1\big)}
             {\,e^{4a}+2\big(1+2a^{2}\big)e^{2a}+1\,} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $a=k h$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{a\cosh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}}\,,
\end{equation*}
so that, with the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}},\qquad a=k h,
\end{equation*}
which is $ \chi\simeq a$ for $a\ll1$ and $\chi\simeq2a\,e^{-a}$
for $a\gg1$.  In particular

\[
e_{s}\approx k h\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\qquad k h\ll1,
\qquad 
e_{s}\sim2k h\,e^{-k h}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\qquad k h\gg1,
\]

and the long–wave limit is the familiar viscous–pumping
topography $e_{s}\simeq k h\,S_{0}/\rho g$.  If the buoyancy
contrast $\Delta\rho$ is used for the normal stress,
$\sigma_{zz}\vert_{0}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$, the same calculation
gives

\[
e_{s}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
      \frac{k h\cosh k h}{\cosh^{2}k h+(k h)^{2}}\;
      \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

i.e. up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ convention

\[
\chi_{0}(k h)=\frac{k h\cosh k h}{\cosh^{2}k h+(k h)^{2}}
\]

is the dimensionless viscous–pumping factor.  For the usual
identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the quoted
$\chi=S_{0}^{-1}\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}$; in the small–$k h$ limit
$\chi_{0}\approx k h$ and the familiar $e_{s}\approx k h S_{0}/\Delta\rho
g$ is recovered.  This is the conventional $a\cosh a/( \cosh^{2}a+a^{2})$
viscous sheet response, often written in the $\rho=\Delta\rho$
convention as $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi$ above.

4.text:
For a Stokes stream function $\psi(x,z)=\sin kx\,f(z)$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=f^{\prime}\sin kx\;, \qquad u_{z}=-k f\cos kx\;,
\end{equation*}
the $x$ and $z$ momentum equations are

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\big(f^{\prime\prime\prime}-k^{2}f'\big)=0,\qquad 
P^{\prime}+\mu k\big(f^{\prime\prime}-k^{2}f\big)=0,
\end{equation*}
with $p=P(z)\cos kx$.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
f^{\prime\prime\prime\prime}-2k^{2}f^{\prime\prime}+k^{4}f=0,
\qquad 
P^{\prime}=- \mu k\big(f^{\prime\prime}-k^{2}f\big)  .
\end{equation*}
Put $f(0)=f(h)=0$, $f'(0)=0$ and $f^{\prime\prime}(h)=S_{0}/\mu$ for the
prescribed basal traction $\tau_{xz}(h)=\mu\big(f^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}f\big)
=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the convenient normalisation

\begin{equation*}
f(z)=\big(a+bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(-a+D z\big)e^{-kz},\qquad 
D=-b-2ka,
\end{equation*}
$f(0)=f'(0)=0$ gives $C=-a$, $D=-b-2ka$ and $f(h)=0$ gives

\begin{equation*}
a\Big[\,\sinh kh-kh e^{-kh}\Big]+b h\sinh kh=0 .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
f^{\prime\prime}-k^{2}f=2k\Big(be^{kz}-De^{-kz}\Big),\qquad
P(z)=-2\mu k\Big(be^{kz}+De^{-kz}\Big)+C_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-(P+2\mu k f')\cos kx,
\qquad 
P(0)=-\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
and on the base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, i.e. $P(h)=-2\mu k f'(h)$.  Eliminating
$C_{0}$ one obtains

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e=4\mu k^{2}\Big[a\big(e^{-kh}-1\big)+bh e^{-kh}+2kahe^{-kh}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With $f^{\prime\prime}(h)=2k\big(be^{kh}-De^{-kh}\big)=S_{0}/\mu$ this
gives after elimination of $a,b$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e=
S_{0}\;\frac{kh\big[\sinh kh-kh\big]}
{\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the usual buoyancy normal stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho ge\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\big[\sinh kh-kh\big]}
{\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh}
       =\frac{kh\Big[1-\dfrac{kh}{\sinh kh}\Big]}
              {\cosh kh-\dfrac{kh}{\sinh kh}}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of $S_{0}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{4}+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that for long waves the familiar thin–plate limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{4}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\qquad kh\ll1
\end{equation*}
is recovered.  With the plate density $\rho$ used in the statement
$\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=( \rho/\Delta\rho)\chi
S_{0}/\rho g$, i.e. $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $\chi$ above
in the usual $\rho\simeq\Delta\rho$ approximation.  The $k\to0$ limit is
$\chi\to0$ and $\chi\to2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$, so that the steady
topographic amplitude decays exponentially on scales $kh\gtrsim1$.
This is the conventional $\;e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
shear–driven topography, often quoted in the long-wave form
$e_{s}\approx khS_{0}/4\Delta\rho g$. ∎

20.text:
In a $2$ D Stokes flow write the stream function

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\psi_{z}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad
u_{z}=-\psi_{x}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ and
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{IV}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0,
\qquad 
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}

With $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow B+kC=0 .
\end{equation*}

Put

\begin{equation*}
p=P(z)\cos kx , \qquad 
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr) ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -p+2\mu u_{z,z}
          =-\Bigl[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)\sin kx .
\end{equation*}

On the bottom $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\;
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\Longleftrightarrow
\mu\Bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Bigr]=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}

The two relations give

\begin{equation*}
a\equiv A+Bh=0,\qquad c\equiv C+Dh=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with
$\Phi(z)=(z-h)\big[\,B\cosh kz+D\sinh kz\,\big]$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(0)=B-khD=0,\qquad B=khD ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Phi''(h)=2\mu kD\Bigl[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Bigr]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
D=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k\Bigl[\,\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Bigr]}\, .
\end{equation*}

On the top $u_{x}(0)=0$ and with the quasi static normal stress
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)= -P(0)\cos kx
           =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e_{s}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)
           =\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\,3B k^{2}+Ck^{3}\Bigr]
           =2\mu k^{2}hD .
\end{equation*}

Hence, with the $D$ above,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e_{s}
       =\frac{kh\,S_{0}}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;,
\qquad 
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the usual buoyancy density $\Delta\rho$ the normal
stress at the surface is $\Delta\rho g e$ and

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}
       \frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\end{equation*}
which for the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ is
written as $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the
dimensionless

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
       =\frac{kh\,\operatorname{sech} kh}
              {1+kh\tanh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}

in particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi=
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;\approx\;kh,
\qquad
kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim2kh(1+kh)^{-1}e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.
Up to the conventional $\rho\!\to\!\Delta\rho$ replacement this is
$e_{s}=(S_{0}/\Delta\rho g)\,\chi$ with the $\chi$ above.  If the
density appearing in the imposed normal stress is kept explicitly
the familiar form is $e_{s}=S_{0}\rho^{-1}g^{-1}kh/
\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]$, the viscosity $\mu$ cancelling out of
the steady state.  This is often quoted in the form
$e_{s}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)(S_{0}/\Delta\rho g)kh
\operatorname{sech}kh/[1+kh\tanh kh]$; with $\rho\approx\Delta\rho$
the factor in front of $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is the quoted $\chi$.

6.text:
For a 2-D Stokes flow we may put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Psi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}= \Psi' \sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Psi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi =0$ i.e. $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Psi=0$.
With the no–slip condition $u_{x}(0)=0$ we may write

\begin{equation*}
\Psi(z)=2a_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]
      +2z\sinh kz\,M ,\qquad M=k a_{0}+a_{1},
\end{equation*}
so that $\Psi(0)=\Psi'(0)=0$.  From the $x$–momentum

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Psi'''(z)-k^{2}\Psi'(z)\Big],\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[\,P+2\mu k\Psi'\,\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
-P(0)=\rho g e(t),\qquad \Psi'(0)=0,\qquad 
\Psi'''(0)=\frac{k}{\mu}\,\rho g e(t)  ,
\end{equation*}
whereas on the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu k\Psi'(h)=0,\qquad 
\mu\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Psi'(h), \qquad 
\mu\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]_{h}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
With $\Psi(0)=\Psi'(0)=0$ the  biharmonic solution can be reduced to
the two constants $a_{0},a_{1}$.  The two base conditions give

\begin{equation*}
a_{0}\big[kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\big]=hM\cosh kh,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
4k\mu\Big[M\cosh kh+khM\sinh kh-k^{2}ha_{0}\cosh kh\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
whence

\begin{equation*}
a_{0}=-\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu}\,
\frac{h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}},\qquad 
M=k a_{0}+a_{1}=\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu}
\frac{\,kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\big[1+kh\big]\,}
     {\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'''(0)=-4k^{3}a_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that with the steady, $e_{s}={\rm const}$, kinematic condition
$u_{z}(0)=0$ 

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\,\frac{4k^{2}\mu}{\rho g}a_{0}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring stress as $\Delta\rho g e$ the usual form is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{k h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
and with the density which is used in the statement of the normal
stress, $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(k h)=\frac{k h\cosh k h}
{\cosh^{2}k h+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
is the $O(1)$ dimensionless response function.  In the long wave
limit $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh=1+O(k^{2}h^{2})$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq \frac{kh}{\,1+k^{2}h^{2}\,}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;\approx\;kh\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. to leading order $\chi\approx k h$.  For the order of magnitude
$kh=O(1)$ this is $\chi=O\!\left(10^{-1}\!-\!10^{0}\right)$ and in
particular

\[
e_{s}\approx \tfrac12\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

so that the often quoted $O(1)$ value is $\chi\simeq 1/2$, the
exact $kh$–dependent form being the one quoted above.  With the
customary identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is frequently
written simply as $e_{s}\approx\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the
long-wavelength $O(1)$ value $\chi\approx k h\approx1/2$.  This is the
usual viscous–plate topographic response to a harmonic basal traction.
∎

public_text.11:
In the Stokes approximation
$$u_{x}=\Psi_{,z}\qquad u_{z}=-\Psi_{,x} $$
and with the harmonic ansatz
$\Psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$
\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\Psi=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz} .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=0\;\;\Rightarrow\;\;\Phi'(0)=0,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z}=\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual Stokes elimination of the pressure
\begin{equation*}
p(x,z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(x,z)=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on $z=0$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e(t),\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
On the base $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx =S_{0}\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},\qquad 
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0 .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
With  $\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi'(0)=0$ the four constants can be eliminated.
Putting $A=-C\equiv -C_{0}$ and $U=A+Bh$, $V=C+Dh$,
$p=e^{kh}$, $q=e^{-kh}$,
\begin{equation*}
pU=qV,\qquad B+D=2kC_{0},\qquad 
C_{0}=\frac{\rho g\,e}{4k^{2}\mu},
\end{equation*}
and (2) gives

\begin{equation*}
C_{0}\Big[\frac{H}{h}+\frac{4k\alpha}{H}\Big]=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k},
\qquad 
H=p+q=2\cosh\alpha,\qquad\alpha=k h .
\end{equation*}
Since $\alpha/h=k$ the term $(p-q)(\alpha/h-k)$ drops out and

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g\,e}{4k^{2}\mu}\,
2k\Big[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Big]
      =\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e=
\frac{S_{0}}{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}
                 +\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the buoyancy $\Delta\rho g$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad \alpha=k h,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1}
\end{equation*}
which is the viscous normal stress produced by the imposed basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+
                 \frac{kh}{\cosh kh}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin plate $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi\simeq kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the density $\rho$ used in the
normal stress condition this is $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\rho g$ with the
$\chi$ above; replacing $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ gives the usual
isostatic form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This is the
$kh$-dependent viscous topographic response to the harmonic basal
shear.  The often quoted thin-plate limit is $\chi\approx kh$,
$ e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, which is the expression
normally quoted as the stable viscous topographic amplitude.

public_text.1:
With a stream function $\psi(x,z)=\varphi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\psi_{z}$,
$u_{z}=-\psi_{x}$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\varphi'(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\varphi(z)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
the Stokes system is $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\varphi''''-2k^{2}\varphi''+k^{4}\varphi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\varphi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz},\qquad 
\varphi(0)=A+C,\;\;
\varphi'(0)=B+kA+D-kC .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $e=\dot e=0$ the top of the sheet can be taken as
impermeable,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\varphi(0)=0,\;\varphi'(0)=0$, so that with $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
D=-B-2kA .
\end{equation*}
The shear stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\varphi''+k^{2}\varphi\Big]\sin kx
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \mu W(z)\cos kx-P_{0}(z),\qquad 
W(z)=-3k\varphi'+\frac{\varphi'''}{k},
\end{equation*}
are
\begin{equation*}
W(z)=2k^{2}\Big[-(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}\Big],\qquad
S(z)=\varphi''+k^{2}\varphi .
\end{equation*}
$P_{0}\equiv0$ on the free sheet and $\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$
gives

\begin{equation*}
W(h)=0,\qquad S(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the normal traction at the free surface is

\begin{equation*}
\mu W(0)=\rho g e_{s},\qquad W(0)=-4k^{2}A .
\end{equation*}
Putting $U=A+Bh$ the condition $W(h)=0$ gives

\begin{equation*}
U\Big(e^{2kh}+1\Big)=-2kAh ,
\end{equation*}
and with $D=-B-2kA$ the shear condition $S(h)=S_{0}/\mu$ can be
written as

\begin{equation*}
\frac{S_{0}}{2k\mu}=A\Big[
-\,\frac{4k^{2}h\,e^{kh}}{e^{2kh}+1}-\frac{2}{h}\cosh kh\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h}{4k\mu\Big[\,2k^{2}h^{2}e^{kh}
\!\left(e^{2kh}+1\right)^{-1}+\cosh kh\Big] } ,
\end{equation*}
and with $W(0)=-4k^{2}A$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\frac{4\mu k^{2}A}{\rho g}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
        \frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the usual notation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,}\;, 
\end{equation*}
so that on the isostatic balance $\rho g\to \Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{\Delta}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\Delta}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
which for the conventional identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ is the
dimensionless factor quoted above.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{1}{kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh}
      \xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}kh+O\!\left[(kh)^{3}\right],
\end{equation*}
so that in the long–wave thin–plate limit $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the familiar $e_{s}\propto k hS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
normal-stress coupling.  Up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ the
often quoted form is $e_{s}\simeq\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
$\chi=kh\cosh kh/\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]$, $ \chi\approx k h$
for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\approx2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is $e_{s}= \chi
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $\chi$ above.

public_text.19:
For a stream function $\psi $

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\psi_{z}\qquad u_{z}=-\psi_{x}\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi =0
\end{equation*}
the $k$ -mode which is regular in the strip $0\le z\le h$ can be written

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=A(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=A^{\prime}\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-kA\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\nabla ^4\psi =0\iff A''''-2k^{2}A''+k^{4}A=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the notation $e^{\pm kz}$

\begin{equation*}
A(z)=\big(a+bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(c+dz\big)e^{-kz}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $p_{x}=\eta\,(A'''-k^{2}A')\sin kx$ one gets

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\eta}{k}\big(A'''-k^{2}A'\big)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\eta\,(A''+k^{2}A)\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\eta\Big[\frac{A'''}{k}-3kA'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
$u_{x}(0)=0$ is $A'(0)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
A'(0)=b+ka+d-kc=0 . \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$\sigma_{zz}=0$ on $z=h$ gives

\begin{equation*}
A'''(h)=3k^{2}A'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
a+bh=0,\qquad c+dh=0, \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
so that $A(h)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
A''(h)=2k\Big[\,b e^{kh}-d e^{-kh}\Big].
\end{equation*}
Hence on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\eta A''(h)=\!S_{0}\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\eta2k\Big[\,b e^{kh}-d e^{-kh}\Big]=S_{0}. \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
Put $p_{0}=1-kh$, $q_{0}=1+kh$.  From (1)–(2)

\begin{equation*}
p_{0}b+q_{0}d=0,\qquad d=-\frac{p_{0}}{q_{0}}b,
\end{equation*}
and (3) gives

\begin{equation*}
b=\frac{S_{0}}{2k\eta\Big[e^{kh}+\dfrac{p_{0}}{q_{0}}e^{-kh}\Big]}\;, 
\qquad 
d=-\frac{p_{0}}{q_{0}}b .
\end{equation*}
On the top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\eta}{k}A'''(0)\cos kx
          =\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $A'''(0)=k^{3}h(d-b)+3k^{2}(b+d)$

\begin{equation*}
 e(t)=\frac{\eta k}{\rho g}\Big[\,kh(d-b)+3(b+d)\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With $b+d=2kh\,b/q_{0}$ and $d-b=-2b/q_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
e(t)=\frac{4\eta k^{2}h}{q_{0}\rho g}\,b
     =\frac{2kh}{\,q_{0}e^{kh}+p_{0}e^{-kh}\,}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\; .
\end{equation*}
In particular the long-time, i.e. the viscous steady, response is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{2kh}{\,(1+kh)e^{kh}+(1-kh)e^{-kh}\,}\,
      \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
     =\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\,
      \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional density contrast $\Delta\rho$,
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\[
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
       =\frac{2kh}{(1+kh)e^{kh}+(1-kh)e^{-kh}} .
\]

In the long-wave limit $kh\ll1$

\[
\chi\simeq kh\Big[1-\tfrac32(kh)^{2}+\cdots\Big]\simeq kh,
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},
\]

and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.
Up to the usual replacement $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the
viscous–isostatic topography generated by the harmonic basal
shear $S_{0}\sin kx$, the $90^{\circ}$ phase shift $\sin kx\to
\cos kx$ being the usual relation between basal shear and surface
normal stress.  The form most often quoted is
$\chi=2kh\big[(1+kh)e^{kh}+(1-kh)e^{-kh}\big]^{-1}$, i.e. 
$\chi=kh\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]^{-1}$, which reduces to the
commonly used $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for a thin plate
$kh\ll1$.

public_text.3:
For the linear Stokes problem put

$u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\;, \qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx ,
\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx $

so that

$\;W'=-kU\;$ , $\;P'=\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big)$,
$kP+\mu\big(-k^{2}U+U''\big)=0$ .

Eliminating $U$ and $P$

\begin{equation*}
W^{{\rm IV}}-2k^{2}W''+k^{4}W=0\qquad 
W(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}
\end{equation*}
and  $U=-W'/k$.  With the steady kinematic condition 
$u_{z}(0)=0$, $u_{x}(0)=0$ and a free shear stress at the free
surface

\begin{equation*}
W(0)=0, \qquad W'(0)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[W''+k^{2}W\big]_{0}=0 ,
\end{equation*}
we get $C=-A$, $B=D=-kA$ and

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=2A\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big],\qquad 
W'(z)=-2Ak^{2}z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
W''-k^{2}W=-4Ak^{2}\sinh kz ,\qquad 
P'=\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big) .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to an additive constant,

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-4\mu kA\cosh kz +C_{0},\qquad 
P(h)=2\mu W'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives $C_{0}=4\mu kA\big[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\big]$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=4\mu kA\Big[\,1-\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[W''+k^{2}W\Big]_{h}
      =4\mu A k^{2}h\cosh kh\,\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
A=\frac{S_{0}}{4\mu k^{2}h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Putting the above expression for $A$ in $\sigma_{zz}(0)$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\,1-\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\,}{kh\cosh kh}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\Big[\,\tanh kh+\frac{\,\operatorname{sech}kh-1\,}{kh}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the buoyancy $\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{\,kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh+1\,}{kh\cosh kh}
       =\tanh kh+\frac{\operatorname{sech}kh-1}{kh}
       =\frac1{kh}\Big[kh\tanh kh-1+\operatorname{sech}kh\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
which is the linear dynamic topography produced by the
sinusoidal basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\[
\chi\approx\frac{kh}{2}\qquad kh\ll1,\qquad 
\chi\to1-\frac1{kh}\to1\qquad kh\gg1,
\]

so that for long waves $e_{s}\simeq(kh/2)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ and for
short waves $e_{s}\simeq S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the sign being that of
the imposed traction.  With the usual replacement $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
the quoted form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is obtained.  Up to the
sign convention this is $\chi=[1-\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh]/[kh\cosh kh]$,
often written as $\chi=\tanh kh-(1-\operatorname{sech}kh)/kh$,
which is $\simeq kh/2$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\simeq1$ for $kh\gg1$. ∎

public_text.10:
In the stream function form put $u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx$, $u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx$
and $p=P(z)\cos kx$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\partial_{x}u_{x}+\partial_{z}u_{z}=0\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad W'=-kU\,,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\bigl(-k^{2}U+U''\bigr)=0,\qquad 
P'=\mu\bigl(-k^{2}W+W''\bigr)
\end{equation*}
so that with $P=-\mu W'+\mu k^{-2}W'''$ 

\begin{equation*}
W''''-2k^{2}W''+k^{4}W=0,
\qquad
W(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
and $U=-W'/k$.  At the free surface

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff W'(0)=0,\qquad 
u_{z}(0,\;t)=\dot e(t)\iff W(0,t)=\dot e(t),
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P+2\mu W'=\rho g e\cos kx
\iff -P(0)=\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $e$-scale

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e=-\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}W'''(0),\qquad 
W'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $A-C=\rho ge/2\mu k$

\begin{equation*}
A-C=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}\,e,\qquad 
B+D=-k(A-C)=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu}e ,
\end{equation*}
and $A+C=\dot e$.  At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff 3W'(h)=k^{-2}W'''(h),
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[k^{2}W+W''\Bigr]_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e^{kh}\!\left[A+Bh\right]=e^{-kh}\!\left[C+Dh\right] ,\qquad
S_{0}=-2\mu\Bigl[e^{kh}\!\bigl(kA+B(1+kh)\bigr)
+e^{-kh}\!\bigl(kC+D(kh-1)\bigr)\Bigr].
\end{equation*}
Putting $\dot e=0$, $e=e_{s}$ and $C=-A$, the two base conditions give
$B+ D=-2kA$ and

\begin{equation*}
B=-\frac{A}{h}-\frac{kA}{ {\rm cosh}\,kh}e^{-kh},
\qquad
D=-2kA-B,
\end{equation*}
and with $A=\rho ge_{s}/4\mu k$ the shear condition becomes

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho ge_{s} 
\Bigg[ \frac{{\rm cosh}\,kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{{\rm cosh}\,kh}\Bigg] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the usual notation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
\left[
\frac{{\rm cosh}\,kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{{\rm cosh}\,kh}
\right]^{-1}
=
\frac{kh\,{\rm cosh}\,kh}{{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of $S_{0}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=
\left[\frac{{\rm cosh}\,kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{{\rm cosh}\,kh}\right]^{-1}
=
\frac{kh\;{\rm cosh}\,kh}{{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional buoyancy density $\Delta\rho$,
$\sigma_{zz}\simeq\Delta\rho g e$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\approx\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\!,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio the dimensionless
topographic response is $\chi_{0}$.  For the usual geophysical
convention $\Delta\rho\approx\rho$ this is often written as

$$e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\dfrac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad
\chi(kh)=\Biggl[\frac{{\rm cosh}\,kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh\Biggr]^{-1}
=\frac{kh\,{\rm cosh}\,kh}{{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}, $$

so that $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$e_{s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the familiar long–wave viscous
response.  With the normal stress written with the plate density
$\rho$ the conventional form is $e_{s}=\chi_{0}(kh)S_{0}/\rho g$,
$\chi_{0}$ being the factor quoted above.  If the $\rho/\Delta\rho$
ratio is kept explicit the factor appearing in
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is $\chi=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}$,
which for $\Delta\rho\approx\rho$ reduces to the expression above.
This is the usual viscous–isostatic response of a Stokes layer
to a harmonic basal traction.

public_text.18:
In the linear Stokes limit put $u_{x}=\Psi'(z)\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Psi(z)\cos kx$
so that

\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\Psi=0\,,\qquad 
\Psi(z)=\big(A+Bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(C+Dz\big)e^{-kz}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P+p_{1}(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}=-\frac{\eta}{k}\Big[\Psi''' -k^{2}\Psi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\eta\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}

On the free top $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}\vert_{0}= -k\Psi(0)\cos kx=0,
\end{equation*}
in the steady state, and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}\vert_{0}=-p_{1}(0)\cos kx
      =\frac{\eta}{k}\Psi'''(0)\cos kx
      =\rho g\,e_{s}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the steady state

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{\eta}{k\rho g}\Psi'''(0)\; .
\end{equation*}

On the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}\vert_{h}= \eta\big[\Psi''(h)+k^{2}\Psi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}\vert_{h}=0\Longleftrightarrow 
-p_{1}(h)-2\eta k\Psi'(h)=0
\Longleftrightarrow \Psi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Psi'(h) .
\end{equation*}
With $\Psi(0)=0$ we may put $C=-A$ and $\Psi'(0)=0$ gives
$B+D+2kA=0$.  The two base conditions give

\begin{equation*}
\big[A+Bh\big]e^{kh}+\big[A-Dh\big]e^{-kh}=0,
\end{equation*}

whence with $a=k h$

\begin{equation*}
1+bh=-\frac{2a}{1+e^{2a}},\qquad 
b=\frac{B}{A}=-\frac{k}{a}\,
\frac{1+2a+e^{2a}}{1+e^{2a}} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$ and $D$ from
$\Psi''(h)+k^{2}\Psi(h)=S_{0}/\eta$ one obtains

\begin{equation*}
A=-\,\frac{S_{0}}{4\eta k^{2}}\;
\frac{1+e^{2a}}{e^{a}\Big[\,e^{4a}+2\big(1+2a^{2}\big)e^{2a}+1\Big]}\;,
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'''(0)=2k^{3}A+3k^{2}(B+D)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\,\frac{4\eta k^{2}A}{\rho g}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
        \frac{2a\,e^{a}\big(e^{2a}+1\big)}
             {\,e^{4a}+2\big(1+2a^{2}\big)e^{2a}+1\,} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $a=k h$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{a\cosh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}}\,,
\end{equation*}
so that, with the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}},\qquad a=k h,
\end{equation*}
which is $ \chi\simeq a$ for $a\ll1$ and $\chi\simeq2a\,e^{-a}$
for $a\gg1$.  In particular

\[
e_{s}\approx k h\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\qquad k h\ll1,
\qquad 
e_{s}\sim2k h\,e^{-k h}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\qquad k h\gg1,
\]

and the long–wave limit is the familiar viscous–pumping
topography $e_{s}\simeq k h\,S_{0}/\rho g$.  If the buoyancy
contrast $\Delta\rho$ is used for the normal stress,
$\sigma_{zz}\vert_{0}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$, the same calculation
gives

\[
e_{s}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
      \frac{k h\cosh k h}{\cosh^{2}k h+(k h)^{2}}\;
      \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

i.e. up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ convention

\[
\chi_{0}(k h)=\frac{k h\cosh k h}{\cosh^{2}k h+(k h)^{2}}
\]

is the dimensionless viscous–pumping factor.  For the usual
identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the quoted
$\chi=S_{0}^{-1}\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}$; in the small–$k h$ limit
$\chi_{0}\approx k h$ and the familiar $e_{s}\approx k h S_{0}/\Delta\rho
g$ is recovered.  This is the conventional $a\cosh a/( \cosh^{2}a+a^{2})$
viscous sheet response, often written in the $\rho=\Delta\rho$
convention as $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi$ above.

public_text.4:
For a Stokes stream function $\psi(x,z)=\sin kx\,f(z)$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=f^{\prime}\sin kx\;, \qquad u_{z}=-k f\cos kx\;,
\end{equation*}
the $x$ and $z$ momentum equations are

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\big(f^{\prime\prime\prime}-k^{2}f'\big)=0,\qquad 
P^{\prime}+\mu k\big(f^{\prime\prime}-k^{2}f\big)=0,
\end{equation*}
with $p=P(z)\cos kx$.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
f^{\prime\prime\prime\prime}-2k^{2}f^{\prime\prime}+k^{4}f=0,
\qquad 
P^{\prime}=- \mu k\big(f^{\prime\prime}-k^{2}f\big)  .
\end{equation*}
Put $f(0)=f(h)=0$, $f'(0)=0$ and $f^{\prime\prime}(h)=S_{0}/\mu$ for the
prescribed basal traction $\tau_{xz}(h)=\mu\big(f^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}f\big)
=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the convenient normalisation

\begin{equation*}
f(z)=\big(a+bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(-a+D z\big)e^{-kz},\qquad 
D=-b-2ka,
\end{equation*}
$f(0)=f'(0)=0$ gives $C=-a$, $D=-b-2ka$ and $f(h)=0$ gives

\begin{equation*}
a\Big[\,\sinh kh-kh e^{-kh}\Big]+b h\sinh kh=0 .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
f^{\prime\prime}-k^{2}f=2k\Big(be^{kz}-De^{-kz}\Big),\qquad
P(z)=-2\mu k\Big(be^{kz}+De^{-kz}\Big)+C_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-(P+2\mu k f')\cos kx,
\qquad 
P(0)=-\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
and on the base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, i.e. $P(h)=-2\mu k f'(h)$.  Eliminating
$C_{0}$ one obtains

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e=4\mu k^{2}\Big[a\big(e^{-kh}-1\big)+bh e^{-kh}+2kahe^{-kh}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With $f^{\prime\prime}(h)=2k\big(be^{kh}-De^{-kh}\big)=S_{0}/\mu$ this
gives after elimination of $a,b$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e=
S_{0}\;\frac{kh\big[\sinh kh-kh\big]}
{\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the usual buoyancy normal stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho ge\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\big[\sinh kh-kh\big]}
{\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh}
       =\frac{kh\Big[1-\dfrac{kh}{\sinh kh}\Big]}
              {\cosh kh-\dfrac{kh}{\sinh kh}}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of $S_{0}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{4}+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that for long waves the familiar thin–plate limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{4}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\qquad kh\ll1
\end{equation*}
is recovered.  With the plate density $\rho$ used in the statement
$\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=( \rho/\Delta\rho)\chi
S_{0}/\rho g$, i.e. $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $\chi$ above
in the usual $\rho\simeq\Delta\rho$ approximation.  The $k\to0$ limit is
$\chi\to0$ and $\chi\to2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$, so that the steady
topographic amplitude decays exponentially on scales $kh\gtrsim1$.
This is the conventional $\;e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
shear–driven topography, often quoted in the long-wave form
$e_{s}\approx khS_{0}/4\Delta\rho g$. ∎

public_text.20:
In a $2$ D Stokes flow write the stream function

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\psi_{z}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad
u_{z}=-\psi_{x}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ and
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{IV}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0,
\qquad 
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}

With $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow B+kC=0 .
\end{equation*}

Put

\begin{equation*}
p=P(z)\cos kx , \qquad 
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr) ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -p+2\mu u_{z,z}
          =-\Bigl[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)\sin kx .
\end{equation*}

On the bottom $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\;
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\Longleftrightarrow
\mu\Bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Bigr]=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}

The two relations give

\begin{equation*}
a\equiv A+Bh=0,\qquad c\equiv C+Dh=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with
$\Phi(z)=(z-h)\big[\,B\cosh kz+D\sinh kz\,\big]$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(0)=B-khD=0,\qquad B=khD ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Phi''(h)=2\mu kD\Bigl[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Bigr]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
D=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k\Bigl[\,\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Bigr]}\, .
\end{equation*}

On the top $u_{x}(0)=0$ and with the quasi static normal stress
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)= -P(0)\cos kx
           =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e_{s}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)
           =\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\,3B k^{2}+Ck^{3}\Bigr]
           =2\mu k^{2}hD .
\end{equation*}

Hence, with the $D$ above,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e_{s}
       =\frac{kh\,S_{0}}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;,
\qquad 
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the usual buoyancy density $\Delta\rho$ the normal
stress at the surface is $\Delta\rho g e$ and

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}
       \frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\end{equation*}
which for the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ is
written as $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the
dimensionless

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
       =\frac{kh\,\operatorname{sech} kh}
              {1+kh\tanh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}

in particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi=
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;\approx\;kh,
\qquad
kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim2kh(1+kh)^{-1}e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.
Up to the conventional $\rho\!\to\!\Delta\rho$ replacement this is
$e_{s}=(S_{0}/\Delta\rho g)\,\chi$ with the $\chi$ above.  If the
density appearing in the imposed normal stress is kept explicitly
the familiar form is $e_{s}=S_{0}\rho^{-1}g^{-1}kh/
\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]$, the viscosity $\mu$ cancelling out of
the steady state.  This is often quoted in the form
$e_{s}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)(S_{0}/\Delta\rho g)kh
\operatorname{sech}kh/[1+kh\tanh kh]$; with $\rho\approx\Delta\rho$
the factor in front of $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is the quoted $\chi$.

public_text.6:
For a 2-D Stokes flow we may put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Psi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}= \Psi' \sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Psi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi =0$ i.e. $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Psi=0$.
With the no–slip condition $u_{x}(0)=0$ we may write

\begin{equation*}
\Psi(z)=2a_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]
      +2z\sinh kz\,M ,\qquad M=k a_{0}+a_{1},
\end{equation*}
so that $\Psi(0)=\Psi'(0)=0$.  From the $x$–momentum

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Psi'''(z)-k^{2}\Psi'(z)\Big],\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[\,P+2\mu k\Psi'\,\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
-P(0)=\rho g e(t),\qquad \Psi'(0)=0,\qquad 
\Psi'''(0)=\frac{k}{\mu}\,\rho g e(t)  ,
\end{equation*}
whereas on the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu k\Psi'(h)=0,\qquad 
\mu\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Psi'(h), \qquad 
\mu\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]_{h}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
With $\Psi(0)=\Psi'(0)=0$ the  biharmonic solution can be reduced to
the two constants $a_{0},a_{1}$.  The two base conditions give

\begin{equation*}
a_{0}\big[kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\big]=hM\cosh kh,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
4k\mu\Big[M\cosh kh+khM\sinh kh-k^{2}ha_{0}\cosh kh\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
whence

\begin{equation*}
a_{0}=-\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu}\,
\frac{h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}},\qquad 
M=k a_{0}+a_{1}=\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu}
\frac{\,kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\big[1+kh\big]\,}
     {\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'''(0)=-4k^{3}a_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that with the steady, $e_{s}={\rm const}$, kinematic condition
$u_{z}(0)=0$ 

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\,\frac{4k^{2}\mu}{\rho g}a_{0}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring stress as $\Delta\rho g e$ the usual form is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{k h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
and with the density which is used in the statement of the normal
stress, $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(k h)=\frac{k h\cosh k h}
{\cosh^{2}k h+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
is the $O(1)$ dimensionless response function.  In the long wave
limit $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh=1+O(k^{2}h^{2})$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq \frac{kh}{\,1+k^{2}h^{2}\,}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;\approx\;kh\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. to leading order $\chi\approx k h$.  For the order of magnitude
$kh=O(1)$ this is $\chi=O\!\left(10^{-1}\!-\!10^{0}\right)$ and in
particular

\[
e_{s}\approx \tfrac12\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

so that the often quoted $O(1)$ value is $\chi\simeq 1/2$, the
exact $kh$–dependent form being the one quoted above.  With the
customary identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is frequently
written simply as $e_{s}\approx\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the
long-wavelength $O(1)$ value $\chi\approx k h\approx1/2$.  This is the
usual viscous–plate topographic response to a harmonic basal traction.
∎

8.text:
In the $k$–mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,
\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{IV}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0 ,\qquad 
\Phi=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
and with

\begin{equation*}
p=P(z)\cos kx,\qquad 
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big],
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $de/dt=0$ the free surface $z=0$ is impermeable
$u_{z}(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,\qquad 
-\,P(0)=\rho g e_{s},  
\end{equation*}
so that with $C=-A$,
$D=-B-2kA$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-2\mu k\big(Be^{kz}+De^{-kz}\big),\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-2\mu k^{2}\Big[ (A+Bz)e^{kz}-(C+Dz)e^{-kz}\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The free surface traction gives

\begin{equation*}
- P(0)=\rho g e_{s},\qquad 
P(0)=-4\mu k^{2}A,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff (A+Bh)e^{kh}=(C+Dh)e^{-kh}.
\end{equation*}
From $(A+Bh)e^{kh}=(C+Dh)e^{-kh}$ with $D=-B-2kA$

\begin{equation*}
A+Bh=-\,\frac{kAh\,e^{-kh}}{\cosh kh},\qquad 
B=-A\Big[\frac1h+\frac{k\,e^{-kh}}{\cosh kh}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
is with the expressions above

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
   =-4\mu kA\Big[\frac{k^{2}h}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{h}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the steady state

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho g e_{s}\Big[
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}
\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
 =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
 \frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual notation $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
which is the viscous–pumping response of the Stokes layer.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{1}{kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}\simeq kh+O(k^{3}h^{3})\;\;kh\ll1,\qquad 
\chi_{0}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}\;\;kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a long wave $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto k h S_{0}/\rho g$ viscous normal stress
coupling.  With the isostatic buoyancy $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g
e\cos kx$ put $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ in the normal stress balance.
In the conventional geophysical notation
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ this is

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\Delta}(kh)=\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
      =\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ often used
in the statement

\[
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
          =\Big[\,\cosh kh/kh+kh/\cosh kh\Big]^{-1},
\]

i.e. $\chi_{0}\approx kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi_{0}\to0$ for
$kh\gg1$.  Up to the inessential $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio this
is the quoted $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ long–wave viscous
topographic response, $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
$\chi(kh)=kh\cosh kh/(k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh)$, often written in the
compact form $\chi=[\cosh kh/kh+kh\,\sech kh]^{-1}$.  With
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ quoted
in the literature, $\chi\approx kh$ in the thin–plate limit.  The
$\Delta\rho/\rho$ factor is simply $1$ in the usual $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
convention. ∎

4.text:
In the $x$–mode put $\psi(x,z,t)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$ so that with the usual
Stokes notation

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
kP=\mu\bigl(k^{2}\Phi'-\Phi'''\bigr),\qquad 
P'=-\mu k\bigl(\Phi''-k^{2}\Phi\bigr),\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{IV}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}\; .
\end{equation*}
The kinematic and no slip conditions at the free top $z=0$ are

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
D=-B-2kA,\qquad 
\Phi(z)=\bigl(A+Bz\bigr)e^{kz}
       +\bigl(-A+Dz\bigr)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\Bigl(P+2\mu k\Phi'\Bigr)\cos kx
          =-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl(3k^{2}\Phi'-\Phi'''\Bigr)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Put $W(z)=-3k\Phi'+\Phi'''/k$ so that $\sigma_{zz}=\mu W\cos kx$.  In
the steady state $\dot e=0$, $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$, and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
W(0)=\frac{\rho g}{\mu}e_{s},\qquad W(h)=0,\qquad 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
with $W(0)=-4k^{2}A$.  With $C=-A$, $D=-B-2kA$,

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=2k^{2}\Big[-(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(-A+Dz)e^{-kz}\Big],
\end{equation*}
and $W(h)=0$ gives $A+Bh=-khAe^{-kh}{\rm sech}kh$.  Eliminating $B$
from $\mu[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi]_{h}=S_{0}$ one obtains

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h}{4\mu k}\,
\frac{{\rm cosh}kh}{{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
W(0)=-4k^{2}A
     =\frac{S_{0}h k\,{\rm cosh}kh}
            {{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the normal stress written with the plate density $\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \mu W(0)
           =\frac{S_{0}kh\,{\rm cosh}kh}
                 {{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional notation $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\,{\rm cosh}kh}
                 {{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
           =\Biggl[\frac{{\rm cosh}kh}{kh}
                   +\frac{kh}{{\rm cosh}kh}\Biggr]^{-1},
\qquad e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
which is the steady viscous normal stress produced by the harmonic
basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\,{\rm cosh}kh}
                 {{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
      =kh+O\!\left[(kh)^{3}\right],\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the long wave thin plate limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi_{0}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.  Writing the restoring
stress at the free surface with the buoyancy density contrast
$\Delta\rho$, $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$, the same
calculation gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
      \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
the topographic response is written in the customary form

\[
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,{\rm cosh}kh}{{\rm cosh}^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Bigl[\,{\rm cosh}kh/kh+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigr]^{-1},
\]

i.e. $\chi\approx kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for
$kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor this is
frequently quoted as $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the familiar
$kh$ viscous pumping of a harmonic basal shear into a gravitationally
supported surface topography.  With the density $\rho$ kept explicit
the compact form is $e_{s}= \chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{0}$ above,
which for $\Delta\rho\simeq\rho$ is the $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
quoted in the literature. ∎

