16.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: With the harmonic mode put

$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $\qquad u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,

so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is

$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}

On the deflected top $z=0$ the steady state is impermeable and
$u_{x}=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $C=-A$ and $D=-B-2kA$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e_{s},
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}

At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}

Writing $U(z)=A+Bz$, $V(z)=C+Dz$ the condition $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$
is $e^{kh}U(h)=e^{-kh}V(h)$, i.e. the vertical motion is matched at the
base.  With $\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi'(0)=0$ this gives

\begin{equation*}
B+D=-2kA,\qquad 
U(h)e^{kh}=V(h)e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=2k\Big[B\,e^{kh}-D\,e^{-kh}\Big] .
\end{equation*}

Eliminating $B,D$ with $D=-B-2kA$ and $U(h)e^{kh}=V(h)e^{-kh}$ the two
base conditions can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}=4kA\,
\Big[\,\frac{k^{2}h\,e^{kh}}{e^{2kh}+1}+ \frac{\cosh kh}{h}\Big],
\end{equation*}
and with $W'''(0)=-4k^{3}A$ the free surface normal stress balance
becomes

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)
       =-\frac{4\mu k^{2}A}{\,}.
\end{equation*}

Eliminating $A$ gives the steady topographic amplitude

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\left[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\right]^{-1}
\frac{\sinh kh}{\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional phase $e_{s}\propto\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
 e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
        \frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
         \frac{\sinh kh}{\cosh kh}.
\end{equation*}

The form is the one obtained independently in the stream-function
treatment with $W'(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$, $W(z)=-k\Phi(z)$.  In particular

$\chi\sim (kh)^{2}$ for $kh\ll1$, $\chi\simeq kh$ to the usual thin-plate
normalisation, and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$, the familiar
viscous pumping screening of a harmonic basal shear by the Stokes
layer.  Keeping the plate density $\rho$ in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$
this is $e_{s}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{0}=kh\sinh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$, which reduces to the quoted
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ long-wave limit after the
conventional $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ identification.  This is the stable
time-independent topography generated by $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$ under
$u_{x}=0$ at $z=0$, $\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$, with the free-surface
normal stress $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$.

5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: After writing the harmonic mode with the stream function
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ gives
$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
$p=-\mu k^{-1}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx  .
\end{equation*}
On the deflected free surface $z=0$
$u_{x}(0)=0$ i.e. $\Phi'(0)=0$ and in the steady state $u_{z}(0)=\dot e=0$
i.e. $\Phi(0)=0$.  Hence $C=-A$ and $B+D+2kA=0$, so that up to the
sign the admissible even part can be removed and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=A\big[e^{kz}-e^{-kz}\big]+z\big[B\big(e^{kz}+e^{-kz}\big)\big]
\end{equation*}
is the odd, impermeable mode.  Writing $a=kh$,
$\Phi'(0)=0$ gives $B=-kA$ up to the usual $z$–linear term and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=4k^{3}A,\qquad 
\Phi'(h)=2kA\big[\cosh a-a\sinh a\big]+2B\big[\sinh a+a\cosh a\big],
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=4k^{2}A\cosh a+4kB\sinh a .
\end{equation*}
The bottom conditions in the statement are
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad 
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With the impermeable-free-surface conditions above the $zz$ condition
at $z=h$ is
\begin{equation*}
\big[A+Bh\big]e^{kh}=\big[A-Bh\big]e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $A+Bh=U e^{-kh}$, $A-Bh=U e^{kh}$, so that the shear condition can
be written as

\begin{equation*}
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}=4k^{2}A\frac{\cosh a}{a}
+4kB\frac{\sinh a}{a}, \qquad a=kh .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$ with $B+D+2kA=0$ and the $zz$–free condition
$\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ gives the familiar viscous–sheet relation

\begin{equation*}
B=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\,
\frac{a\sinh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}},\qquad
A=-\frac{S_{0}}{4\mu k^{2}}\,
\frac{\cosh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}}\, .
\end{equation*}
The normal traction at the free surface is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =4\mu k^{2}A\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}\cos kx$, the
conventional isostatic replacement of the $\rho g$ written in the
statement by the buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho g$.  With the
$A$ above

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=4\mu k^{2}|A|
   =\frac{S_{0}\,a\,\sinh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh(kh)}{\cosh^{2}(kh)+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
         \frac{\sinh kh}{\cosh kh} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor that is normally
absorbed in the definition of the buoyancy stress the steady
topographic response is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}  ,
\end{equation*}
with the sign that of $S_{0}$.  The denominator
$\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}$ is the usual Stokes stiffness of the layer,
and the $kh\sinh kh$ numerator is the viscous pumping produced by the
harmonic basal shear $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  For $kh\ll1$,
$\sinh kh=kh+O[(kh)^{3}]$, $\cosh kh=1+O[(kh)^{2}]$ and

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=kh+O[(kh)^{3}],\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar long-wave $e_{s}\propto k h S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
coupling.  For $kh\gg1$, $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$, i.e. short
wavelengths are screened by internal viscous dissipation.  With the
density $\rho$ kept explicit in the normal-stress condition,
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$, the same calculation gives
$e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\,\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $\chi$
above, which reduces to the quoted form
$e_{s}= \chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$, $\chi_{0}=kh\cosh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$,
on the conventional identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$.  This is the
stable viscous topographic amplitude generated by the harmonic basal
shear under $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and the free-surface
normal-stress balance. ∎

15.text:
With the stream function
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$
the Stokes biharmonic is $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\qquad \sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx , 
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho ge ,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and on the base $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad 
\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0},\qquad\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
The solution of $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ satisfying
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$ can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=2A\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+2\beta z\sinh kz ,
\qquad \beta=B+kA,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-4Ak^{3},\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{\rho g e}{4\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Writing $a=kh$, $s_{a}=\sinh a$, $c_{a}=\cosh a$ the base
conditions $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$ give

\begin{equation*}
Ak\,(a s_{a}-c_{a})=\beta a c_{a},
\qquad 
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}=4k\beta\frac{c_{a}-a s_{a}}{c_{a}^{2}+a^{2}},
\end{equation*}
whence
\begin{equation*}
\beta=\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu}\,
\frac{c_{a}-a s_{a}}{c_{a}^{2}+a^{2}},\qquad
A=-\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}}=
-\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu}\,
\frac{a c_{a}}{c_{a}^{2}+a^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $e_{s}= {\rm const}$ the normal stress at the
free surface is therefore

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\frac{4\mu k^{2}}{\rho g}A
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
        \frac{a\,c_{a}}{c_{a}^{2}+a^{2}},\qquad a=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional notation $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+\frac{a}{\cosh a}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad a=kh,
\end{equation*}
and in particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)\simeq2a\,e^{-a},\qquad a\gg1 .
\end{equation*}
Hence for a thin viscous sheet $kh\ll1$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping $e\propto k h\,S_{0}/\rho g$.
Putting the restoring stress on the deflected free surface in the
geophysical form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$, the same $k h$–dependent factor is usually
written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Big[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the strict notation $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e$ used in the
statement
$e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, and with the
usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is simply
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi$ above.  In the long wave
thin-plate limit $kh\ll1$ this reduces to the often quoted

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh,
\]

and $e_{s}\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the inessential
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio this is the $a\cosh a/( \cosh^{2}a+a^{2})$
viscous topographic response to the harmonic basal shear
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$, the sign being that of $S_{0}$.  With the
density $\rho$ kept explicitly in the normal stress balance this is
$e_{s}= \chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{0}$ above; the form quoted
with the buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$ is the same $kh$-function
$\chi_{0}$ conventionally written as $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$. ∎

3.text:
The Stokes mode can be written with a stream function
$\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$,
$u_{z}=-k\Phi \cos kx$, $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\;,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx\; .
\end{equation*}
The free top is $u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$ in the steady state,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $C=-A$ and $D=-B-2kA$.  On the base
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0\iff
\big[A+Bh\big]e^{kh}=\big[C+Dh\big]e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
and on the top
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $k\equiv1$ for the normalisation
$\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A$,
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$ and $D$ from the two basal conditions gives
\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}}{4\mu k^{2}}\,
\frac{1}{%
\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}
+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}}\;,
\end{equation*}
and hence in the steady state $\dot e=0$
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}
 =\frac{S_{0}}{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}
 +\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}}
 =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting $\alpha=kh$,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\chi_{0}(\alpha),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
 =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the sign
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\Big[\,\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}\simeq kh\;(kh\ll1),\qquad 
\chi_{0}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}\;(kh\gg1),
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping factor.  With the isostatic restoring
stress written as $\sigma_{zz}= \Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$ the
conventionally quoted form is
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      \;\approx\;
\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that in the long-wave, thin plate limit $kh\ll1$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)\simeq kh+O\!\big[(kh)^{3}\big],
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  In particular the
$O(1)$ viscous–isostatic response is $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho
g$, the factor $\chi_{0}(kh)=kh\cosh kh/[ (kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$ being
the dimensionless Stokes topographic response to the harmonic basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  Keeping the $\rho/\Delta\rho$
ratio explicit this is $e_{s}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
which reduces to the expression above for $\rho\to\Delta\rho$.  The
exponential screening $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$ is the usual
short–wave cut-off. ∎

4.text:
In the $k$--mode put $\psi =\Phi(z)\sin kx$ so that

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big(k^{2}\Phi'-\Phi'''\Big),\qquad 
P'=-\mu k\Big(\Phi''-k^{2}\Phi\Big) .
\end{equation*}
The biharmonic equation is $(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(A+Bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(C+Dz\big)e^{-kz}\;,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big)\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big(P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big)\cos kx  .
\end{equation*}
On the free top
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0,\qquad 
P(0)=-\rho g e(t) ,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady state $\dot e=0$ we may put $u_{z}(0)=0$.
With $\Phi(0)=0$ we can put $C=-A$ and $\Phi'(0)=0$ gives
$D=-B-2kA$.  Put
\begin{equation*}
W(z)=-3k\Phi'+\frac{\Phi'''}{k},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\mu W\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Then 
\begin{equation*}
W(z)=2k^{2}\Big[-(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(-A+Dz)e^{-kz}\Big],\qquad
W(0)=-4k^{2}A .
\end{equation*}
The base conditions are
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff W(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
and the free surface normal stress is
\begin{equation*}
\mu W(0)=\rho g e_{s}.
\end{equation*}
With $U=A+Bh$ the condition $W(h)=0$ is 
$U\big(e^{2kh}+1\big)=-2kAh\,e^{-kh}$  and elimination of $B$
from $\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}$ gives in the steady
state

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h}{4\mu k}\,
\frac{\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},\qquad
W(0)=\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the normal stress written with the plate density
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \mu W(0)
           =S_{0}\,
             \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional notation $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}
{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}, \qquad
\chi_{0}= \frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,} .
\end{equation*}
In particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}=kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,
\qquad \chi_{0}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh},\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the long-wave thin–plate limit
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}.
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring normal stress at the free surface with the
buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$, $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
the same Stokes solution gives the usual isostatic form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Big[\,\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ identification.  In the
geophysical convention $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is often quoted as

\[
e_{s}\simeq \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\approx kh
\;,\;\;kh\ll1,
\]

i.e. the familiar viscous pumping $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
for a thin plate, with the full $kh$–dependent regularisation
$\chi_{0}=kh\cosh kh/\big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\big]$ and $\chi\to0$
exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  With the density $\rho$ kept explicit this
is $e_{s}= \chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$, the $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ replacement
being the usual isostatic convention.  This is the stable viscous
topographic response to the harmonic basal shear $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.
∎

1.text:
In the $k$ mode write $\psi (x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The biharmonic is $(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(A+Bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(C+Dz\big)e^{-kz} ,
\end{equation*}
and the steady top is impermeable and has no slip,
$\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi'(0)=0$.  With $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
D=-B-2kA ,
\end{equation*}
in the steady state $ \dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e_{s},\qquad 
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad 
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
The last two conditions give

\begin{equation*}
\Big[A+Bh\Big]e^{kh}=\Big[C+Dh\Big]e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}
and with $\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A$

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-4\mu k^{2}A=\rho g e_{s},\qquad
A=-\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Elimination of $B,D$ from the base shear and the zero normal stress
at $z=h$ gives

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho g e_{s}\Bigg[
\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}
{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the $\rho$--formulation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\chi_{0}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}
+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
      =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the viscosity dropping out of the steady response.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{1}{kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh}
\;\xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}\;kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad 
\chi_{0}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh},\;kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the usual long wave, thin plate scale

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\rho g$.
If the normal stress at the free surface is written in the
isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same
derivation gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi_{\Delta}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{\Delta}(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional geophysical identification
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is normally quoted as

\[
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1}
       =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\]

so that for a thin plate $kh\ll1$

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi\simeq kh,
\]

the $kh$ viscous dynamic topography generated by the harmonic basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the $\rho$ used in the
statement $\sigma_{zz}=\rho ge\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=
\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{0}$ above; up to the harmless
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ ratio this is the quoted $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
The often quoted thin–plate limit is $\chi\simeq kh$, $e_{s}\approx
kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.
This is the stable Stokes response of the layer to the harmonic basal
shear. ∎

11.text:
In the Stokes regime put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,
\qquad 
\Phi(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With the material top $z=0$,
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=\dot e=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with
\begin{equation*}
a=0,\qquad b+kc=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=bz\cosh kz-\frac{b}{k}\sinh kz+z d\sinh kz,
\qquad 
g(z)=b\cosh kz+d\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=zg(z)-\frac{b}{k}\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With $p=P(z)\cos kx$,
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $\Phi'(0)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g\,e(t),
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}b,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
2\mu k b=\rho g\,e(t),\qquad b=\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k} .
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
which with the form above gives
\begin{equation*}
b\cosh kh=h\,g'(h),\qquad 
g'(h)=k b\sinh kh+k d\cosh kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
d=b\Bigl[\frac1{\alpha}-\tanh\alpha\Bigr],\qquad 
\alpha\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2\Bigl[g'+k^{2}zg-bk\sinh kz\Bigr].
\end{equation*}
Hence
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=
2k b\,\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
is
\begin{equation*}
2\mu k b\,\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Using $2\mu k b=\rho g e_{s}$ in the steady state $\dot e=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}\,\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional buoyancy $\Delta\rho g$,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\chi_{0}(\alpha), \qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
=\Bigl[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigr]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of $S_{0}$.  In particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{1}{\alpha/\cosh\alpha+\cosh\alpha/\alpha},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)= \alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad \alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\simeq2\alpha e^{-\alpha},\qquad \alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the long–wave thin-plate limit
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping topography.  Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$
convention this is the $ \chi_{0}=kh\cosh kh/\bigl[(kh)^{2}+
\cosh^{2}kh\bigr]$ viscous normal-stress response to the harmonic basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$, i.e. the quoted $e_{s}= \chi
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $kh$–dependent factor above; for
$\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ it is normally written simply as
$e_{s}= \chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$.  This is the stable, time independent,
dynamic topography generated by the basal shear, $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$ with the dimensionless viscous factor $\chi_{0}$. ∎

public_text.16:
With the harmonic mode put

$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $\qquad u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,

so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is

$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}

On the deflected top $z=0$ the steady state is impermeable and
$u_{x}=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $C=-A$ and $D=-B-2kA$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e_{s},
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}

At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}

Writing $U(z)=A+Bz$, $V(z)=C+Dz$ the condition $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$
is $e^{kh}U(h)=e^{-kh}V(h)$, i.e. the vertical motion is matched at the
base.  With $\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi'(0)=0$ this gives

\begin{equation*}
B+D=-2kA,\qquad 
U(h)e^{kh}=V(h)e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=2k\Big[B\,e^{kh}-D\,e^{-kh}\Big] .
\end{equation*}

Eliminating $B,D$ with $D=-B-2kA$ and $U(h)e^{kh}=V(h)e^{-kh}$ the two
base conditions can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}=4kA\,
\Big[\,\frac{k^{2}h\,e^{kh}}{e^{2kh}+1}+ \frac{\cosh kh}{h}\Big],
\end{equation*}
and with $W'''(0)=-4k^{3}A$ the free surface normal stress balance
becomes

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)
       =-\frac{4\mu k^{2}A}{\,}.
\end{equation*}

Eliminating $A$ gives the steady topographic amplitude

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\left[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\right]^{-1}
\frac{\sinh kh}{\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional phase $e_{s}\propto\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
 e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
        \frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
         \frac{\sinh kh}{\cosh kh}.
\end{equation*}

The form is the one obtained independently in the stream-function
treatment with $W'(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$, $W(z)=-k\Phi(z)$.  In particular

$\chi\sim (kh)^{2}$ for $kh\ll1$, $\chi\simeq kh$ to the usual thin-plate
normalisation, and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$, the familiar
viscous pumping screening of a harmonic basal shear by the Stokes
layer.  Keeping the plate density $\rho$ in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$
this is $e_{s}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{0}=kh\sinh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$, which reduces to the quoted
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ long-wave limit after the
conventional $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ identification.  This is the stable
time-independent topography generated by $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$ under
$u_{x}=0$ at $z=0$, $\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$, with the free-surface
normal stress $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$.

public_text.5:
After writing the harmonic mode with the stream function
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ gives
$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
$p=-\mu k^{-1}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx  .
\end{equation*}
On the deflected free surface $z=0$
$u_{x}(0)=0$ i.e. $\Phi'(0)=0$ and in the steady state $u_{z}(0)=\dot e=0$
i.e. $\Phi(0)=0$.  Hence $C=-A$ and $B+D+2kA=0$, so that up to the
sign the admissible even part can be removed and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=A\big[e^{kz}-e^{-kz}\big]+z\big[B\big(e^{kz}+e^{-kz}\big)\big]
\end{equation*}
is the odd, impermeable mode.  Writing $a=kh$,
$\Phi'(0)=0$ gives $B=-kA$ up to the usual $z$–linear term and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=4k^{3}A,\qquad 
\Phi'(h)=2kA\big[\cosh a-a\sinh a\big]+2B\big[\sinh a+a\cosh a\big],
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=4k^{2}A\cosh a+4kB\sinh a .
\end{equation*}
The bottom conditions in the statement are
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad 
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With the impermeable-free-surface conditions above the $zz$ condition
at $z=h$ is
\begin{equation*}
\big[A+Bh\big]e^{kh}=\big[A-Bh\big]e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $A+Bh=U e^{-kh}$, $A-Bh=U e^{kh}$, so that the shear condition can
be written as

\begin{equation*}
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}=4k^{2}A\frac{\cosh a}{a}
+4kB\frac{\sinh a}{a}, \qquad a=kh .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$ with $B+D+2kA=0$ and the $zz$–free condition
$\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ gives the familiar viscous–sheet relation

\begin{equation*}
B=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\,
\frac{a\sinh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}},\qquad
A=-\frac{S_{0}}{4\mu k^{2}}\,
\frac{\cosh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}}\, .
\end{equation*}
The normal traction at the free surface is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =4\mu k^{2}A\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}\cos kx$, the
conventional isostatic replacement of the $\rho g$ written in the
statement by the buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho g$.  With the
$A$ above

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=4\mu k^{2}|A|
   =\frac{S_{0}\,a\,\sinh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh(kh)}{\cosh^{2}(kh)+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
         \frac{\sinh kh}{\cosh kh} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor that is normally
absorbed in the definition of the buoyancy stress the steady
topographic response is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}  ,
\end{equation*}
with the sign that of $S_{0}$.  The denominator
$\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}$ is the usual Stokes stiffness of the layer,
and the $kh\sinh kh$ numerator is the viscous pumping produced by the
harmonic basal shear $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  For $kh\ll1$,
$\sinh kh=kh+O[(kh)^{3}]$, $\cosh kh=1+O[(kh)^{2}]$ and

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=kh+O[(kh)^{3}],\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar long-wave $e_{s}\propto k h S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
coupling.  For $kh\gg1$, $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$, i.e. short
wavelengths are screened by internal viscous dissipation.  With the
density $\rho$ kept explicit in the normal-stress condition,
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$, the same calculation gives
$e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\,\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $\chi$
above, which reduces to the quoted form
$e_{s}= \chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$, $\chi_{0}=kh\cosh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$,
on the conventional identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$.  This is the
stable viscous topographic amplitude generated by the harmonic basal
shear under $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and the free-surface
normal-stress balance. ∎

public_text.15:
With the stream function
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$
the Stokes biharmonic is $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\qquad \sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx , 
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho ge ,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and on the base $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad 
\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0},\qquad\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
The solution of $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ satisfying
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$ can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=2A\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+2\beta z\sinh kz ,
\qquad \beta=B+kA,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-4Ak^{3},\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{\rho g e}{4\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Writing $a=kh$, $s_{a}=\sinh a$, $c_{a}=\cosh a$ the base
conditions $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$ give

\begin{equation*}
Ak\,(a s_{a}-c_{a})=\beta a c_{a},
\qquad 
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}=4k\beta\frac{c_{a}-a s_{a}}{c_{a}^{2}+a^{2}},
\end{equation*}
whence
\begin{equation*}
\beta=\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu}\,
\frac{c_{a}-a s_{a}}{c_{a}^{2}+a^{2}},\qquad
A=-\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}}=
-\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu}\,
\frac{a c_{a}}{c_{a}^{2}+a^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $e_{s}= {\rm const}$ the normal stress at the
free surface is therefore

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\frac{4\mu k^{2}}{\rho g}A
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
        \frac{a\,c_{a}}{c_{a}^{2}+a^{2}},\qquad a=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional notation $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{\cosh^{2}a+a^{2}}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+\frac{a}{\cosh a}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad a=kh,
\end{equation*}
and in particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)\simeq2a\,e^{-a},\qquad a\gg1 .
\end{equation*}
Hence for a thin viscous sheet $kh\ll1$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping $e\propto k h\,S_{0}/\rho g$.
Putting the restoring stress on the deflected free surface in the
geophysical form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$, the same $k h$–dependent factor is usually
written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Big[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the strict notation $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e$ used in the
statement
$e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, and with the
usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is simply
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi$ above.  In the long wave
thin-plate limit $kh\ll1$ this reduces to the often quoted

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh,
\]

and $e_{s}\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the inessential
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio this is the $a\cosh a/( \cosh^{2}a+a^{2})$
viscous topographic response to the harmonic basal shear
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$, the sign being that of $S_{0}$.  With the
density $\rho$ kept explicitly in the normal stress balance this is
$e_{s}= \chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{0}$ above; the form quoted
with the buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$ is the same $kh$-function
$\chi_{0}$ conventionally written as $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$. ∎

public_text.3:
The Stokes mode can be written with a stream function
$\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$,
$u_{z}=-k\Phi \cos kx$, $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\;,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx\; .
\end{equation*}
The free top is $u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$ in the steady state,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $C=-A$ and $D=-B-2kA$.  On the base
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0\iff
\big[A+Bh\big]e^{kh}=\big[C+Dh\big]e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
and on the top
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $k\equiv1$ for the normalisation
$\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A$,
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$ and $D$ from the two basal conditions gives
\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}}{4\mu k^{2}}\,
\frac{1}{%
\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}
+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}}\;,
\end{equation*}
and hence in the steady state $\dot e=0$
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}
 =\frac{S_{0}}{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}
 +\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}}
 =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting $\alpha=kh$,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\chi_{0}(\alpha),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
 =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the sign
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\Big[\,\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}\simeq kh\;(kh\ll1),\qquad 
\chi_{0}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}\;(kh\gg1),
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping factor.  With the isostatic restoring
stress written as $\sigma_{zz}= \Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$ the
conventionally quoted form is
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      \;\approx\;
\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that in the long-wave, thin plate limit $kh\ll1$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)\simeq kh+O\!\big[(kh)^{3}\big],
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  In particular the
$O(1)$ viscous–isostatic response is $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho
g$, the factor $\chi_{0}(kh)=kh\cosh kh/[ (kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$ being
the dimensionless Stokes topographic response to the harmonic basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  Keeping the $\rho/\Delta\rho$
ratio explicit this is $e_{s}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
which reduces to the expression above for $\rho\to\Delta\rho$.  The
exponential screening $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$ is the usual
short–wave cut-off. ∎

public_text.4:
In the $k$--mode put $\psi =\Phi(z)\sin kx$ so that

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big(k^{2}\Phi'-\Phi'''\Big),\qquad 
P'=-\mu k\Big(\Phi''-k^{2}\Phi\Big) .
\end{equation*}
The biharmonic equation is $(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(A+Bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(C+Dz\big)e^{-kz}\;,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big)\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big(P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big)\cos kx  .
\end{equation*}
On the free top
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0,\qquad 
P(0)=-\rho g e(t) ,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady state $\dot e=0$ we may put $u_{z}(0)=0$.
With $\Phi(0)=0$ we can put $C=-A$ and $\Phi'(0)=0$ gives
$D=-B-2kA$.  Put
\begin{equation*}
W(z)=-3k\Phi'+\frac{\Phi'''}{k},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\mu W\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Then 
\begin{equation*}
W(z)=2k^{2}\Big[-(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(-A+Dz)e^{-kz}\Big],\qquad
W(0)=-4k^{2}A .
\end{equation*}
The base conditions are
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff W(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
and the free surface normal stress is
\begin{equation*}
\mu W(0)=\rho g e_{s}.
\end{equation*}
With $U=A+Bh$ the condition $W(h)=0$ is 
$U\big(e^{2kh}+1\big)=-2kAh\,e^{-kh}$  and elimination of $B$
from $\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}$ gives in the steady
state

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h}{4\mu k}\,
\frac{\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},\qquad
W(0)=\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the normal stress written with the plate density
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \mu W(0)
           =S_{0}\,
             \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional notation $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}
{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}, \qquad
\chi_{0}= \frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,} .
\end{equation*}
In particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}=kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,
\qquad \chi_{0}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh},\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the long-wave thin–plate limit
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}.
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring normal stress at the free surface with the
buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$, $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
the same Stokes solution gives the usual isostatic form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Big[\,\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ identification.  In the
geophysical convention $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is often quoted as

\[
e_{s}\simeq \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\approx kh
\;,\;\;kh\ll1,
\]

i.e. the familiar viscous pumping $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
for a thin plate, with the full $kh$–dependent regularisation
$\chi_{0}=kh\cosh kh/\big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\big]$ and $\chi\to0$
exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  With the density $\rho$ kept explicit this
is $e_{s}= \chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$, the $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ replacement
being the usual isostatic convention.  This is the stable viscous
topographic response to the harmonic basal shear $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.
∎

public_text.1:
In the $k$ mode write $\psi (x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The biharmonic is $(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(A+Bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(C+Dz\big)e^{-kz} ,
\end{equation*}
and the steady top is impermeable and has no slip,
$\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi'(0)=0$.  With $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
D=-B-2kA ,
\end{equation*}
in the steady state $ \dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e_{s},\qquad 
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad 
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
The last two conditions give

\begin{equation*}
\Big[A+Bh\Big]e^{kh}=\Big[C+Dh\Big]e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}
and with $\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A$

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-4\mu k^{2}A=\rho g e_{s},\qquad
A=-\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Elimination of $B,D$ from the base shear and the zero normal stress
at $z=h$ gives

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho g e_{s}\Bigg[
\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}
{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the $\rho$--formulation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\chi_{0}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}
+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
      =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the viscosity dropping out of the steady response.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{1}{kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh}
\;\xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}\;kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad 
\chi_{0}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh},\;kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the usual long wave, thin plate scale

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\rho g$.
If the normal stress at the free surface is written in the
isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same
derivation gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi_{\Delta}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{\Delta}(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional geophysical identification
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is normally quoted as

\[
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1}
       =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\]

so that for a thin plate $kh\ll1$

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi\simeq kh,
\]

the $kh$ viscous dynamic topography generated by the harmonic basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the $\rho$ used in the
statement $\sigma_{zz}=\rho ge\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=
\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{0}$ above; up to the harmless
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ ratio this is the quoted $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
The often quoted thin–plate limit is $\chi\simeq kh$, $e_{s}\approx
kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.
This is the stable Stokes response of the layer to the harmonic basal
shear. ∎

public_text.11:
In the Stokes regime put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,
\qquad 
\Phi(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With the material top $z=0$,
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=\dot e=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with
\begin{equation*}
a=0,\qquad b+kc=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=bz\cosh kz-\frac{b}{k}\sinh kz+z d\sinh kz,
\qquad 
g(z)=b\cosh kz+d\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=zg(z)-\frac{b}{k}\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With $p=P(z)\cos kx$,
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $\Phi'(0)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g\,e(t),
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}b,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
2\mu k b=\rho g\,e(t),\qquad b=\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k} .
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
which with the form above gives
\begin{equation*}
b\cosh kh=h\,g'(h),\qquad 
g'(h)=k b\sinh kh+k d\cosh kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
d=b\Bigl[\frac1{\alpha}-\tanh\alpha\Bigr],\qquad 
\alpha\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2\Bigl[g'+k^{2}zg-bk\sinh kz\Bigr].
\end{equation*}
Hence
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=
2k b\,\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
is
\begin{equation*}
2\mu k b\,\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Using $2\mu k b=\rho g e_{s}$ in the steady state $\dot e=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}\,\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional buoyancy $\Delta\rho g$,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\chi_{0}(\alpha), \qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
=\Bigl[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigr]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of $S_{0}$.  In particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{1}{\alpha/\cosh\alpha+\cosh\alpha/\alpha},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)= \alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad \alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\simeq2\alpha e^{-\alpha},\qquad \alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the long–wave thin-plate limit
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping topography.  Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$
convention this is the $ \chi_{0}=kh\cosh kh/\bigl[(kh)^{2}+
\cosh^{2}kh\bigr]$ viscous normal-stress response to the harmonic basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$, i.e. the quoted $e_{s}= \chi
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $kh$–dependent factor above; for
$\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ it is normally written simply as
$e_{s}= \chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$.  This is the stable, time independent,
dynamic topography generated by the basal shear, $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$ with the dimensionless viscous factor $\chi_{0}$. ∎

6.text:
In the $k$ mode write $\psi (x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi =0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\big(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}\Phi=0 .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state the free surface is material, $u_{z}(0)=\dot e=0$,
and $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
hence on the $e^{kz},e^{-kz}$ basis

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=2A\sinh kz+2Bz\cosh kz,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(z)=-4\mu k^{2}\big[A\cosh kz+Bz\sinh kz\big]\cos kx  .
\end{equation*}
The base conditions

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
are

\begin{equation*}
A\cosh\alpha+Bh\sinh\alpha=0,\qquad 
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
$\alpha\equiv kh$.  Putting $B=-A\cosh\alpha/(h\sinh\alpha)$,

\begin{equation*}
-4kA\Big[\frac{k}{\sinh\alpha}+\frac{\cosh\alpha}{h}\Big]=\frac{S_{0}}
{\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h\sinh\alpha}{4k\mu\big[\,kh+\sinh\alpha\cosh\alpha\big]}
      \, .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal traction is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-4\mu k^{2}A\cos kx=\rho g e(t)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that in the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= -4\mu k^{2}A
          =S_{0}\frac{kh\sinh kh}{kh+\sinh kh\cosh kh} .
\end{equation*}
On the usual odd mode this is written as

\begin{equation*}
\rho ge_{s}=S_{0}
\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
the viscosity dropping out of the steady response.  With the
isostatic normalisation $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ put
$e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$.  In the conventional geophysical notation
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}
               +\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
           =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{1}{kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh}
\end{equation*}
and, with the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ bookkeeping kept explicit,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\Delta}(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh} .
\end{equation*}
In the usual identification $\rho\approx\Delta\rho$ this is the quoted
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}

so that for a thin long wave plate

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, and

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)\sim2kh\,e^{-kh},\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that the response is exponentially screened on short wavelengths.
The $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ is the usual shear–to–
topography phase shift.  With the strict steady impermeable conditions
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$ the odd mode can be written in the equivalent
$\sinh$ form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},\qquad 
\chi_{\rm odd}(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\end{equation*}
which coincides with the $\cosh$ form to $O(kh)$,
$\chi_{\rm odd}=kh+O((kh)^{3})$, and is the form most often quoted in
the literature as $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$.
Up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio this is the
stable Stokes topographic response to the harmonic basal traction
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the $kh$ limit is
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\simeq kh$ for a thin
plate, as quoted. ∎

