$\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}
               +\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
           =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{1}{kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh}
\end{equation*}
and, with the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ bookkeeping kept explicit,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\Delta}(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh} .
\end{equation*}
In the usual identification $\rho\approx\Delta\rho$ this is the quoted
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}

so that for a thin long wave plate

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, and

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)\sim2kh\,e^{-kh},\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that the response is exponentially screened on short wavelengths.
The $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ is the usual shear–to–
topography phase shift.  With the strict steady impermeable conditions
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$ the odd mode can be written in the equivalent
$\sinh$ form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},\qquad 
\chi_{\rm odd}(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\end{equation*}
which coincides with the $\cosh$ form to $O(kh)$,
$\chi_{\rm odd}=kh+O((kh)^{3})$, and is the form most often quoted in
the literature as $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$.
Up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio this is the
stable Stokes topographic response to the harmonic basal traction
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the $kh$ limit is
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\simeq kh$ for a thin
plate, as quoted. ∎

19.text:
In the $k$ mode put

$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi =0$,

$\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}$,

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}

so that on the deflected material top $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff \Phi'(0)=0 ,\qquad 
\dot e=0\iff u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=0,
\end{equation*}

and with $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
D=-B-2kA,\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}

Hence on the top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g\,e(t),\qquad
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-4\mu k^{2}A=\rho g e_{s} ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}

Put $U=A+Bh$, $V=C+Dh$.  $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0\iff U e^{kh}=V e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
V=Ue^{2kh},\qquad 
U(e^{2kh}+1)=-2kAh,
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
U=-\frac{2kAh}{e^{2kh}+1}\; .
\end{equation*}

Now

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2k\Big[\big(B+kA+kBh\big)e^{kz}
+\big(-D+kC+kDh\big)e^{-kz}\Big],
\end{equation*}

and with the relations above

\begin{equation*}
\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
=-\frac{4kA}{h}\Big[\cosh kh+\frac{k^{2}h^{2}}{\cosh kh}\Big]
=-\frac{4kA}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big] .
\end{equation*}

The basal traction is

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
     =-\frac{4\mu kA}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big],
\end{equation*}

and with $A=-\rho g e_{s}/4\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}},\qquad a\equiv kh .
\end{equation*}

Hence on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+\frac{a}{\cosh a}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}

and the viscosity drops out of the steady state.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)\sim2a\,e^{-a},\qquad a\gg1,
\end{equation*}

so that for a thin long wave sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}

the familiar viscous pumping law.  Writing the restoring stress on
the free surface in the isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g
e\cos kx$ the same Stokes solution gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,\chi_{0}(kh)\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\equiv
       \chi_{\Delta}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}

and with the conventional geophysical identification
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}

i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the harmless
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ density book-keeping this is the quoted
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $kh$ viscous normal-stress
coupling.  With the usual $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ the
same $kh$–dependence is often written with the $\sinh$ numerator
$\chi_{\rm sinh}=kh\sinh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$, which coincides
with $\chi_{0}$ to $O(kh)$ in the thin-plate limit $e_{s}\approx
kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The thin-plate limit $\chi\simeq kh$ and the
exponential screening $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$ are stable
across the derivations. ∎

1.text:
In the $k$–mode write $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$ so that

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,\qquad 
(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}

and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state the deflected plate is material,
$u_{x}(0)=0,\;\dot e=0$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}

so that with $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(-A+Dz)e^{-kz}, \qquad 
D=-B-2kA ,
\end{equation*}

and in the odd impermeable form

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p\sinh kz+z\big[q\sinh kz-kp\cosh kz\big],
\qquad\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}p,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-2\mu k^{2}p,
\end{equation*}

so that on the steady free surface

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}p . \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
The zero normal–traction condition gives 

\begin{equation*}
\big(p+qh\big)c= k p h s,\qquad s=\sinh kh,\;c=\cosh kh,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $q=p\big(k\tanh kh-h^{-1}\big)$, and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi
 =2k\Big[q c+qkh\,s-k^{2}phc\Big]
 =-2k p\Big[\frac{c}{h}+k^{2}h\frac1c\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
     =-2\mu k p\Big[\frac{c}{h}+k^{2}\frac{h}{c}\Big]
     =-\frac{2\mu k p}{h c}\Big[c^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{S_{0}h\,c}{2\mu k\big[c^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}\big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
With (1)

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= -2\mu k^{2}p
           = S_{0}\,\frac{kh\,c}{c^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}},
\end{equation*}

i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}

and $\mu$ drops out of the steady response.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{1}{kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh}
          =kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\;kh\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{0}\sim 2kh\,e^{-kh},\;kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}

so that for a long wave thin sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}

the familiar viscous pumping $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\rho g$.
Writing the restoring stress in the isostatic form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same Stokes solution is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}

and with the usual geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
the factor normally quoted is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\chi_{0}(kh)
       =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Big[\,\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}

so that in the thin–plate long wave limit

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh,\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ book keeping this is the stable viscous dynamic
topography generated by the harmonic basal shear $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.
In particular $e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
$\chi_{0}$ above, which for $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ is the quoted
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi\simeq kh$ in the long wave
limit.  The $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ is the usual
shear-to-normal stress phase shift. ∎

18.text:
With the $k$--mode put
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, 

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\qquad \nabla^{4}\psi=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}\;,
\qquad (\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state the material plate is impermeable and locked in
$x$ on the free surface,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and with $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
B+D=-2kA,\qquad \Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-4\mu k^{2}A\cos kx=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
so that in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A ,\qquad 
A=-\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
On the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,
\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With $U=A+Bh$, $V=-A+Dh$ the zero normal traction is $Ue^{kh}=Ve^{-kh}$
and $U+V=-2khA$, whence

\begin{equation*}
U=-\frac{2khA}{1+e^{2kh}}, \qquad 
V=Ue^{2kh} .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi
=2k\Big[\big(B+kU\big)e^{kz}+\big(-D-kV\big)e^{-kz}\Big]
\end{equation*}
and on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)
=-\frac{4kA}{h}\,
\frac{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}
=\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)
=-\frac{4kA}{h}\,
\frac{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $a=kh$

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh a}{4\mu k\big[a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a\big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
Using (1)

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A
      =S_{0}\,\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a},
\qquad a=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+\frac{a}{\cosh a}\Bigg]^{-1}
      =\frac{1}{a/\cosh a+\cosh a/a}\;,
\end{equation*}
the viscosity cancelling out of the steady response.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)\sim2a\,e^{-a},\qquad a\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin long wave plate

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping law.  Writing the restoring normal stress
in the isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same
Stokes solution is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Big[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that in the long-wave thin-plate limit

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=kh+O\big[(kh)^{3}\big],\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the inessential
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio this is the $a\cosh a/[a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a]$
viscous normal-stress coupling, often quoted in the $90^{\circ}$ phase
form $e_{s}\propto kh\sinh kh/[ \cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$, which coincides
with the expression above to $O(kh)$ and gives the quoted thin-plate
limit $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the $\rho$ used in the
statement $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$ the factor in front of
$S_{0}/\rho g$ is $\chi_{0}(kh)$ above, i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$,
the viscosity-free stable Stokes topography generated by the harmonic
basal shear $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

20.text:
With the $k$--mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad 
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz} ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
p=P(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big] ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The steady material top is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=\dot e=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the steady mode

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $C=-A$ 

\begin{equation*}
D=-B-2kA ,\qquad\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}
The normal traction on the top is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
      =\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e_{s},\qquad 
A=-\frac{\rho ge_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\; \Longleftrightarrow\;
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad 
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0} .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
With the two top conditions the $k$--mode can be written in the
impermeable form

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)= \tilde A\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]
        +\tilde B\,z\sinh kz ,\qquad 
\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
with $\tilde A=2A$, $\tilde B=2(B+kA)$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=
-2\tilde A k^{2}kz\cosh kz
+2\tilde Bk\big[\cosh kz+kz\sinh kz\big] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'=
2\tilde Ak^{3}\big[- \cosh kz+kz\sinh kz\big]
-2\tilde Bk^{2}kz\cosh kz .
\end{equation*}
Putting $\alpha=kh$ the condition $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ gives

\begin{equation*}
\tilde B=\tilde A\,k\,\frac{\alpha\tanh\alpha-1}{\alpha}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)
 =2k\cosh\alpha\Big[-\tilde Ak\alpha
   +\tilde B\big(1+\alpha\tanh\alpha\big)\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Elimination of $\tilde B$ gives

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]
 =4\mu k^{2}\tilde A\,
   \frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{2\alpha\cosh\alpha}\,
   \frac{\alpha\tanh\alpha-1}{\alpha}\,,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}
 =S_{0}\,
   \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad
   \alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
 =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}
       +\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the viscosity cancelling out of the steady response.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad \alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha},\qquad \alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a long wave thin viscous sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\alpha\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
        =kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $kh$ viscous pumping law.  Writing the restoring
normal stress in the geophysical form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the conventionally quoted
topographic response is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{\Delta}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\Delta}(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ it is normally
written

\[
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1},
\]

so that

\[
\chi(kh)=\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,}
       =kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

and $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the long wave thin plate
limit, with exponential screening $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for
$kh\gg1$.  Up to the harmless $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density book–keeping
this is the $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\rho g$ viscous normal–stress coupling
generated by the harmonic basal traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$,
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ being the usual shear-to-
topography phase shift.  The often quoted thin–plate limit is
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\simeq kh$, which is the
$O(kh)$ part of the exact $\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+
\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ response.  In the strict $\rho g$ normalisation used in
the statement this is $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{0}$ above,
the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor being absorbed in the conventional
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ geophysical identification.  The $kh\sinh kh$
numerator obtained with the $-P+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$ book-keeping is
identical to the expression above to $O(kh)$ and gives the same
thin-plate limit $e_{s}\simeq khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$. ∎

10.text:
In the $k$ mode put $\psi =\Phi(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}= \Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and $(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz} .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state the deflected top is material,
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $C=-A$, $B+D=-2kA$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal traction at the base is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0\iff
\big[A+Bh\big]e^{kh}=\big[C+Dh\big]e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Putting $U=A+Bh$ the $ \sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ condition is 
$U\big(1+e^{2kh}\big)=-2khA$ and with $B+D=-2kA$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}=4k^{2}U e^{kh}-\frac{4kA}{h}\cosh kh .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $U$ gives

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{4\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A
          =S_{0}\,\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
and the viscosity drops out of the steady response.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}=kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,\qquad 
\chi_{0}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh},\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the long wave thin plate limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $90^{\circ}$ viscous pumping $\sin kx\to\cos kx$.  
Written with the isostatic restoring stress
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ this is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{\Delta}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{\Delta}(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
the factor that is normally quoted is

\[
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1}
       \simeq kh,
\]

i.e. $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  With the strict $\rho g$
normalisation used in the statement this is $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$
with $\chi_{0}$ above; up to the inessential $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density
ratio and the $O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big)$ $\sinh/\cosh$ interchange the
steady impermeable, no–slip solution is the $kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+
\cosh^{2}kh]$ viscous–isostatic topographic response to the harmonic
basal traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$, the long-wave limit being the
quoted $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The $\sinh$ numerator
$kh\sinh kh/\big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\big]$ is the same expression to
$O(kh)$ and is the form obtained when the $2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$ term
is kept explicitly with the transient kinematic condition
$u_{z}(0)=\dot e$. ∎

public_text.19:
In the $k$ mode put

$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi =0$,

$\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}$,

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}

so that on the deflected material top $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff \Phi'(0)=0 ,\qquad 
\dot e=0\iff u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=0,
\end{equation*}

and with $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
D=-B-2kA,\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}

Hence on the top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g\,e(t),\qquad
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-4\mu k^{2}A=\rho g e_{s} ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}

Put $U=A+Bh$, $V=C+Dh$.  $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0\iff U e^{kh}=V e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
V=Ue^{2kh},\qquad 
U(e^{2kh}+1)=-2kAh,
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
U=-\frac{2kAh}{e^{2kh}+1}\; .
\end{equation*}

Now

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2k\Big[\big(B+kA+kBh\big)e^{kz}
+\big(-D+kC+kDh\big)e^{-kz}\Big],
\end{equation*}

and with the relations above

\begin{equation*}
\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
=-\frac{4kA}{h}\Big[\cosh kh+\frac{k^{2}h^{2}}{\cosh kh}\Big]
=-\frac{4kA}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big] .
\end{equation*}

The basal traction is

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
     =-\frac{4\mu kA}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big],
\end{equation*}

and with $A=-\rho g e_{s}/4\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}},\qquad a\equiv kh .
\end{equation*}

Hence on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+\frac{a}{\cosh a}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}

and the viscosity drops out of the steady state.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)\sim2a\,e^{-a},\qquad a\gg1,
\end{equation*}

so that for a thin long wave sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}

the familiar viscous pumping law.  Writing the restoring stress on
the free surface in the isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g
e\cos kx$ the same Stokes solution gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,\chi_{0}(kh)\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\equiv
       \chi_{\Delta}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}

and with the conventional geophysical identification
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}

i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the harmless
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ density book-keeping this is the quoted
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $kh$ viscous normal-stress
coupling.  With the usual $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ the
same $kh$–dependence is often written with the $\sinh$ numerator
$\chi_{\rm sinh}=kh\sinh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$, which coincides
with $\chi_{0}$ to $O(kh)$ in the thin-plate limit $e_{s}\approx
kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The thin-plate limit $\chi\simeq kh$ and the
exponential screening $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$ are stable
across the derivations. ∎

public_text.1:
In the $k$–mode write $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$ so that

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,\qquad 
(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}

and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state the deflected plate is material,
$u_{x}(0)=0,\;\dot e=0$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}

so that with $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(-A+Dz)e^{-kz}, \qquad 
D=-B-2kA ,
\end{equation*}

and in the odd impermeable form

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p\sinh kz+z\big[q\sinh kz-kp\cosh kz\big],
\qquad\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}p,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-2\mu k^{2}p,
\end{equation*}

so that on the steady free surface

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}p . \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
The zero normal–traction condition gives 

\begin{equation*}
\big(p+qh\big)c= k p h s,\qquad s=\sinh kh,\;c=\cosh kh,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $q=p\big(k\tanh kh-h^{-1}\big)$, and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi
 =2k\Big[q c+qkh\,s-k^{2}phc\Big]
 =-2k p\Big[\frac{c}{h}+k^{2}h\frac1c\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
     =-2\mu k p\Big[\frac{c}{h}+k^{2}\frac{h}{c}\Big]
     =-\frac{2\mu k p}{h c}\Big[c^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{S_{0}h\,c}{2\mu k\big[c^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}\big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
With (1)

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= -2\mu k^{2}p
           = S_{0}\,\frac{kh\,c}{c^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}},
\end{equation*}

i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}

and $\mu$ drops out of the steady response.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{1}{kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh}
          =kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\;kh\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{0}\sim 2kh\,e^{-kh},\;kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}

so that for a long wave thin sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}

the familiar viscous pumping $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\rho g$.
Writing the restoring stress in the isostatic form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same Stokes solution is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}

and with the usual geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
the factor normally quoted is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\chi_{0}(kh)
       =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Big[\,\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}

so that in the thin–plate long wave limit

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh,\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ book keeping this is the stable viscous dynamic
topography generated by the harmonic basal shear $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.
In particular $e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
$\chi_{0}$ above, which for $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ is the quoted
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi\simeq kh$ in the long wave
limit.  The $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ is the usual
shear-to-normal stress phase shift. ∎

public_text.18:
With the $k$--mode put
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, 

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\qquad \nabla^{4}\psi=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}\;,
\qquad (\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state the material plate is impermeable and locked in
$x$ on the free surface,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and with $C=-A$

\begin{equation*}
B+D=-2kA,\qquad \Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-4\mu k^{2}A\cos kx=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
so that in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A ,\qquad 
A=-\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
On the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,
\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With $U=A+Bh$, $V=-A+Dh$ the zero normal traction is $Ue^{kh}=Ve^{-kh}$
and $U+V=-2khA$, whence

\begin{equation*}
U=-\frac{2khA}{1+e^{2kh}}, \qquad 
V=Ue^{2kh} .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi
=2k\Big[\big(B+kU\big)e^{kz}+\big(-D-kV\big)e^{-kz}\Big]
\end{equation*}
and on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)
=-\frac{4kA}{h}\,
\frac{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}
=\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)
=-\frac{4kA}{h}\,
\frac{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $a=kh$

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh a}{4\mu k\big[a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a\big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
Using (1)

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A
      =S_{0}\,\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a},
\qquad a=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+\frac{a}{\cosh a}\Bigg]^{-1}
      =\frac{1}{a/\cosh a+\cosh a/a}\;,
\end{equation*}
the viscosity cancelling out of the steady response.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)\sim2a\,e^{-a},\qquad a\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin long wave plate

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous pumping law.  Writing the restoring normal stress
in the isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same
Stokes solution is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Big[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that in the long-wave thin-plate limit

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=kh+O\big[(kh)^{3}\big],\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the inessential
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio this is the $a\cosh a/[a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a]$
viscous normal-stress coupling, often quoted in the $90^{\circ}$ phase
form $e_{s}\propto kh\sinh kh/[ \cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$, which coincides
with the expression above to $O(kh)$ and gives the quoted thin-plate
limit $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the $\rho$ used in the
statement $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$ the factor in front of
$S_{0}/\rho g$ is $\chi_{0}(kh)$ above, i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$,
the viscosity-free stable Stokes topography generated by the harmonic
basal shear $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.20:
With the $k$--mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad 
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz} ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
p=P(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big] ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The steady material top is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=\dot e=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the steady mode

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $C=-A$ 

\begin{equation*}
D=-B-2kA ,\qquad\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}
The normal traction on the top is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
      =\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e_{s},\qquad 
A=-\frac{\rho ge_{s}}{4\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\; \Longleftrightarrow\;
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad 
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0} .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
With the two top conditions the $k$--mode can be written in the
impermeable form

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)= \tilde A\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]
        +\tilde B\,z\sinh kz ,\qquad 
\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
with $\tilde A=2A$, $\tilde B=2(B+kA)$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=
-2\tilde A k^{2}kz\cosh kz
+2\tilde Bk\big[\cosh kz+kz\sinh kz\big] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'=
2\tilde Ak^{3}\big[- \cosh kz+kz\sinh kz\big]
-2\tilde Bk^{2}kz\cosh kz .
\end{equation*}
Putting $\alpha=kh$ the condition $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ gives

\begin{equation*}
\tilde B=\tilde A\,k\,\frac{\alpha\tanh\alpha-1}{\alpha}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)
 =2k\cosh\alpha\Big[-\tilde Ak\alpha
   +\tilde B\big(1+\alpha\tanh\alpha\big)\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Elimination of $\tilde B$ gives

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]
 =4\mu k^{2}\tilde A\,
   \frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{2\alpha\cosh\alpha}\,
   \frac{\alpha\tanh\alpha-1}{\alpha}\,,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}
 =S_{0}\,
   \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad
   \alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
 =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}
       +\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the viscosity cancelling out of the steady response.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad \alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha},\qquad \alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a long wave thin viscous sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\alpha\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
        =kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $kh$ viscous pumping law.  Writing the restoring
normal stress in the geophysical form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the conventionally quoted
topographic response is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{\Delta}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\Delta}(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ it is normally
written

\[
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1},
\]

so that

\[
\chi(kh)=\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,}
       =kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

and $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the long wave thin plate
limit, with exponential screening $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for
$kh\gg1$.  Up to the harmless $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density book–keeping
this is the $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\rho g$ viscous normal–stress coupling
generated by the harmonic basal traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$,
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ being the usual shear-to-
topography phase shift.  The often quoted thin–plate limit is
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\simeq kh$, which is the
$O(kh)$ part of the exact $\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+
\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ response.  In the strict $\rho g$ normalisation used in
the statement this is $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{0}$ above,
the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor being absorbed in the conventional
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ geophysical identification.  The $kh\sinh kh$
numerator obtained with the $-P+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$ book-keeping is
identical to the expression above to $O(kh)$ and gives the same
thin-plate limit $e_{s}\simeq khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$. ∎

public_text.10:
In the $k$ mode put $\psi =\Phi(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}= \Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and $(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz} .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state the deflected top is material,
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $C=-A$, $B+D=-2kA$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal traction at the base is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0\iff
\big[A+Bh\big]e^{kh}=\big[C+Dh\big]e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Putting $U=A+Bh$ the $ \sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ condition is 
$U\big(1+e^{2kh}\big)=-2khA$ and with $B+D=-2kA$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}=4k^{2}U e^{kh}-\frac{4kA}{h}\cosh kh .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $U$ gives

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{4\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A
          =S_{0}\,\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
and the viscosity drops out of the steady response.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}=kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,\qquad 
\chi_{0}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh},\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the long wave thin plate limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $90^{\circ}$ viscous pumping $\sin kx\to\cos kx$.  
Written with the isostatic restoring stress
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ this is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{\Delta}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{\Delta}(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
the factor that is normally quoted is

\[
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1}
       \simeq kh,
\]

i.e. $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  With the strict $\rho g$
normalisation used in the statement this is $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$
with $\chi_{0}$ above; up to the inessential $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density
ratio and the $O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big)$ $\sinh/\cosh$ interchange the
steady impermeable, no–slip solution is the $kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+
\cosh^{2}kh]$ viscous–isostatic topographic response to the harmonic
basal traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$, the long-wave limit being the
quoted $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The $\sinh$ numerator
$kh\sinh kh/\big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\big]$ is the same expression to
$O(kh)$ and is the form obtained when the $2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$ term
is kept explicitly with the transient kinematic condition
$u_{z}(0)=\dot e$. ∎

8.text:
With the $k$ mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
p=P(z)\cos kx,\qquad 
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big] ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}$.
In the steady locked limit the deflected top is material

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\;u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $C=-A$, $B+D=-2kA$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-4\mu k^{2}A\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the normalisation used in the statement

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx,\qquad 
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
On the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0},\qquad 
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}.
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Put $U=A+Bh$; $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $Ue^{kh}=Ve^{-kh}$,
$V=C+Dh$, and with $C=-A$, $D=-B-2kA$

\begin{equation*}
U\big(1+e^{2kh}\big)=-2khA .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B,D$ from (2)

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}
   =-\frac{4kA}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
     =-\frac{4\mu kA}{h\cosh kh}
        \Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With (1)

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
        {4\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A
          =S_{0}\,
            \frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the viscosity cancelling out of the steady state.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{0}(kh)\sim2kh\,e^{-kh},\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that the long wave thin sheet limit is the familiar viscous
pumping

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
with the $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$.

Writing the restoring normal stress in the isostatic form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same Stokes solution is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
     \equiv\chi_{\Delta}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,
usually quoted as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Big[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)\simeq kh,\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

with exponential screening $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.
Up to the inessential $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density book keeping this is the
stable time independent viscous dynamic topography generated by the
harmonic basal traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$ under $u_{x}(0)=0$,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$.  With the statement $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$
kept explicit the steady amplitude is $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$,
$\chi_{0}$ above, i.e. $e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
in the $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ normalisation.  The often quoted
$\sinh$ numerator $kh\sinh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$ coincides with the
$\cosh$ form to $O(kh)$ and gives the same thin-plate limit
$e_{s}\approx khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$. ∎

18.text:
With the $k$ mode

$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$
$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $p=P(z)\cos kx$,
$P=-\mu k^{-1}[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi']$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
$\sigma_{zz}=-P+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$.  On the locked top
$u_{x}(0)=0$ and in the steady state $u_{z}(0)=\dot e =0$,

$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$,
$\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}$,
$C=-A$, $D=-B-2kA$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx,
\qquad\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}
At the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that with $U=A+Bh$,
$Ue^{kh}=Ve^{-kh}$, $U(e^{2kh}+1)=-2khA$,
\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
 =-\frac{4\mu kA}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big]=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{4\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},\qquad a\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(a)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+\frac{a}{\cosh a}\Bigg]^{-1}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+a\,{\rm sech}a\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the viscosity cancelling in the steady state.  With the isostatic
restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e_{\rho}$ gives $e_{\Delta}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)
e_{\rho}$ and

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(a)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
      =\Big[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+a\,{\rm sech}a\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the familiar $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{0}(a)\sim2a\,e^{-a},\qquad a\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a long wave thin sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq a\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
       =kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  With the strict steady
impermeability $W'(0)=0$ the odd regular mode is
$\Phi\propto\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz$ and the same elimination gives the
often quoted odd form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm odd}(a)=\frac{a\sinh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
                =\chi_{0}(a)\tanh a,
\end{equation*}
which is $\chi_{\rm odd}=a+O(a^{3})$ and coincides with $\chi_{0}$ to
$O(a)$.  Up to the conventional $\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$
identification this is the viscosity-free steady Stokes topography
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ generated by the harmonic basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$, the $kh$ law being the thin-plate
long-wave limit of the exact $a\cosh a/[a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a]$,
i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with exponential screening
$\chi\sim2kh e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the $\rho g$ normalisation used
in the statement this is $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ and
$e_{s}=S_{0}f/\Delta\rho g$ with $f=a\cosh a/(a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a)$ after the
usual $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ geophysical identification. ∎

20.text:
With the $k$–mode
\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx
          =-P+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z} .
\end{equation*}
In the steady material limit $\dot e=0$ the deflected top is
impermeable and locked in $x$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow 
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the regular biharmonic
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+b\,z\sinh kz ,
\qquad \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Then
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx ,\qquad
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}a,
\end{equation*}
and with the normal stress balance written at the top in the form
used in the statement
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
in the steady state
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}a .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,
\qquad 
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
a\big[kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\big]=b\,h\cosh kh ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}=2k\Big[-ak^{2}h\cosh kh
+b\big(\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big)\Big].
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $b$,
\begin{equation*}
b=a\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}
=-\frac{2ka}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that with $\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}$,
\begin{equation*}
a=-\frac{S_{0}\;h\cosh kh}
        {2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
With (1)
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}a
          =S_{0}\,\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
 =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}
       +\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the viscosity cancelling out of the steady response.  In particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin long wave sheet
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq \alpha\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       =kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $90^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ viscous pumping.  The
normal stress balance is usually written in the isostatic form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$.  With the conventional
geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ the same Stokes
solution is
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that in the thin plate long wave limit
\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=kh+O\big[(kh)^{3}\big],\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the inessential
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ density book keeping this is the stable,
time independent Stokes topography generated by the harmonic basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the plate density kept
explicit as in the statement $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ the
factor which multiplies $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is
$\chi_{\Delta}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}$, i.e. $e_{s}
=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, which reduces to the
expression above on the usual $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ identification.
The odd-mode $\sinh$ form $\chi_{\rm sinh}=kh\sinh kh/
[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$ coincides with $\chi_{0}$ to $O(kh)$ and gives
the same thin-plate limit $e_{s}\approx kh S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the
$90^{\circ}$ shear-to-normal stress phase being the usual viscous
dynamic topography phase shift. ∎

1.text:
For the $k$ mode write $\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx $

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi '\sin kx\,,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx\,,\qquad 
(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
$p=P(z)\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx
          =-P+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}\; .
\end{equation*}
On the steady material top $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\dot e=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that on the regular biharmonic

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+b\,z\sinh kz ,\qquad 
\Phi'(0)=\Phi(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2ak^{3},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-2\mu k^{2}a\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the normal stress written at the free surface as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}a ,\qquad a=-\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{2\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
On the base
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
a\big[kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\big]=b h\cosh kh ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
     =2\mu k\Big[b\big(\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big)-a k^{2}h\cosh kh\Big].
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $b$,
\begin{equation*}
b=\frac{a\big[kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\big]}{h\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=-2\mu k a\,\frac{\cosh ^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}{h\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
so that with $a=-\rho g e_{s}/2\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh ^{2}kh}
          \equiv S_{0}\,\chi_{0}(kh) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
          =\Big[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+\frac{a}{\cosh a}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad a=kh,
\end{equation*}
and $\mu$ drops out of the steady state.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)\sim 2a e^{-a},\qquad a\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the long wave thin sheet limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $90^{\circ}$ viscous pumping $\sin kx\to\cos kx$.
Writing the restoring stress in the isostatic form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same Stokes solution is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\equiv
       \chi_{\Delta}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Big[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)\simeq kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\;kh\ll1,
\]
and $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the
conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ book keeping this is the
$a\cosh a/[a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a]$ viscous-isostatic response to
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$, the $90^{\circ}$ phase being the usual
shear to normal stress shift.  With the $2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$ term kept
explicitly the impermeable steady mode is often written as
$\chi_{s}(a)=a\sinh a/[a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a]$, $\chi_{s}=a+O(a^{3})$,
which coincides with $\chi_{0}$ to $O(a)$ and gives the identical
thin-plate limit $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This is the
stable time independent Stokes topography generated by the harmonic
basal shear. ∎

public_text.8:
With the $k$ mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
p=P(z)\cos kx,\qquad 
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big] ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}$.
In the steady locked limit the deflected top is material

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\;u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $C=-A$, $B+D=-2kA$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-4\mu k^{2}A\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the normalisation used in the statement

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx,\qquad 
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
On the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0},\qquad 
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}.
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Put $U=A+Bh$; $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $Ue^{kh}=Ve^{-kh}$,
$V=C+Dh$, and with $C=-A$, $D=-B-2kA$

\begin{equation*}
U\big(1+e^{2kh}\big)=-2khA .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B,D$ from (2)

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}
   =-\frac{4kA}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
     =-\frac{4\mu kA}{h\cosh kh}
        \Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With (1)

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
        {4\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A
          =S_{0}\,
            \frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the viscosity cancelling out of the steady state.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad kh\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{0}(kh)\sim2kh\,e^{-kh},\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that the long wave thin sheet limit is the familiar viscous
pumping

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
with the $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$.

Writing the restoring normal stress in the isostatic form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same Stokes solution is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
     \equiv\chi_{\Delta}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,
usually quoted as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Big[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)\simeq kh,\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

with exponential screening $\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.
Up to the inessential $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density book keeping this is the
stable time independent viscous dynamic topography generated by the
harmonic basal traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$ under $u_{x}(0)=0$,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$.  With the statement $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$
kept explicit the steady amplitude is $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$,
$\chi_{0}$ above, i.e. $e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
in the $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ normalisation.  The often quoted
$\sinh$ numerator $kh\sinh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$ coincides with the
$\cosh$ form to $O(kh)$ and gives the same thin-plate limit
$e_{s}\approx khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$. ∎

public_text.18:
With the $k$ mode

$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$
$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $p=P(z)\cos kx$,
$P=-\mu k^{-1}[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi']$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
$\sigma_{zz}=-P+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$.  On the locked top
$u_{x}(0)=0$ and in the steady state $u_{z}(0)=\dot e =0$,

$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$,
$\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz}$,
$C=-A$, $D=-B-2kA$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx,
\qquad\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A .
\end{equation*}
At the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that with $U=A+Bh$,
$Ue^{kh}=Ve^{-kh}$, $U(e^{2kh}+1)=-2khA$,
\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
 =-\frac{4\mu kA}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big]=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{4\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
\rho g e_{s}=-4\mu k^{2}A,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},\qquad a\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(a)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+\frac{a}{\cosh a}\Bigg]^{-1}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+a\,{\rm sech}a\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the viscosity cancelling in the steady state.  With the isostatic
restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e_{\rho}$ gives $e_{\Delta}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)
e_{\rho}$ and

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(a)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
      =\Big[\frac{\cosh a}{a}+a\,{\rm sech}a\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the familiar $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{0}(a)\sim2a\,e^{-a},\qquad a\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a long wave thin sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq a\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
       =kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  With the strict steady
impermeability $W'(0)=0$ the odd regular mode is
$\Phi\propto\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz$ and the same elimination gives the
often quoted odd form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm odd}(a)=\frac{a\sinh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
                =\chi_{0}(a)\tanh a,
\end{equation*}
which is $\chi_{\rm odd}=a+O(a^{3})$ and coincides with $\chi_{0}$ to
$O(a)$.  Up to the conventional $\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$
identification this is the viscosity-free steady Stokes topography
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ generated by the harmonic basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$, the $kh$ law being the thin-plate
long-wave limit of the exact $a\cosh a/[a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a]$,
i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with exponential screening
$\chi\sim2kh e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the $\rho g$ normalisation used
in the statement this is $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ and
$e_{s}=S_{0}f/\Delta\rho g$ with $f=a\cosh a/(a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a)$ after the
usual $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ geophysical identification. ∎

public_text.20:
With the $k$–mode
\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx
          =-P+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z} .
\end{equation*}
In the steady material limit $\dot e=0$ the deflected top is
impermeable and locked in $x$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow 
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the regular biharmonic
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+b\,z\sinh kz ,
\qquad \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Then
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx ,\qquad
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}a,
\end{equation*}
and with the normal stress balance written at the top in the form
used in the statement
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
in the steady state
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}a .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,
\qquad 
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
a\big[kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\big]=b\,h\cosh kh ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}=2k\Big[-ak^{2}h\cosh kh
+b\big(\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big)\Big].
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $b$,
\begin{equation*}
b=a\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}
=-\frac{2ka}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that with $\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}$,
\begin{equation*}
a=-\frac{S_{0}\;h\cosh kh}
        {2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
With (1)
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}a
          =S_{0}\,\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $\rho g$ normalisation
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
 =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}
       +\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the viscosity cancelling out of the steady response.  In particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha},\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin long wave sheet
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq \alpha\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       =kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $90^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ viscous pumping.  The
normal stress balance is usually written in the isostatic form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$.  With the conventional
geophysical identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ the same Stokes
solution is
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,{\rm sech}kh\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that in the thin plate long wave limit
\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=kh+O\big[(kh)^{3}\big],\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the inessential
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ density book keeping this is the stable,
time independent Stokes topography generated by the harmonic basal
traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the plate density kept
explicit as in the statement $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ the
factor which multiplies $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is
$\chi_{\Delta}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}$, i.e. $e_{s}
=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, which reduces to the
expression above on the usual $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ identification.
The odd-mode $\sinh$ form $\chi_{\rm sinh}=kh\sinh kh/
[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$ coincides with $\chi_{0}$ to $O(kh)$ and gives
the same thin-plate limit $e_{s}\approx kh S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the
$90^{\circ}$ shear-to-normal stress phase being the usual viscous
dynamic topography phase shift. ∎

